help\f[R]
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] is a tool for easily building customized OS images.
It\(cqs a fancy wrapper around \f[B]dnf\f[R], \f[B]apt\f[R],
\f[B]pacman\f[R] and \f[B]zypper\f[R] that may generate disk images with
a number of bells and whistles.
.SS Command Line Verbs
The following command line verbs are known:
.TP
\f[B]init\f[R]
Initialize \f[B]mkosi\f[R].
This is a one time operation that sets up various config files required
for an optimal experience.
Currently this only initialized a \f[B]tmpfiles.d\f[R] dropin for the
mkosi package cache directory to make sure old, unused files are cleaned
up automatically.
.TP
\f[B]summary\f[R]
Show a human\-readable summary of all options used for building the
images.
This will parse the command line and configuration files, but only print
what it is configured for and not actually build or run anything.
.TP
\f[B]cat\-config\f[R]
Output the names and contents of all loaded configuration files.
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] loads a bunch of files from different locations and this
command makes it easier to figure out what is configured where.
.TP
\f[B]build\f[R]
Build the image\-based on the settings passed on the command line and in
the configuration files.
This command is the default if no verb is specified.
Arguments may be passed to the build scripts, if some are defined.
To pass options to the build scripts, separate them from regular mkosi
options with \f[B]\-\-\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]shell\f[R]
This builds the image if it is not built yet, and then invokes
\f[B]systemd\-nspawn\f[R] to run an interactive shell in the image.
This doesn\(cqt require booting the system, it\(cqs like a better
chroot.
An optional command line may be specified after the \f[B]shell\f[R]
verb, to be invoked in place of the shell in the container.
To pass extra options to nspawn, separate them from regular options with
\f[B]\-\-\f[R].
.RS
To use \f[B]directory\f[R] images with \f[B]shell\f[R], the
\f[B]ForeignUIDRange=\f[R] option has to be enabled when building the
image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]boot\f[R]
Similar to \f[B]shell\f[R], but instead of spawning a shell, it boots
systemd in the image using \f[B]systemd\-nspawn\f[R].
Extra arguments may be specified after the \f[B]boot\f[R] verb, which
are passed as the \f[I]kernel command line\f[R] to the init system in
the image.
To pass extra options to nspawn, separate them from regular options with
\f[B]\-\-\f[R].
.RS
To use \f[B]directory\f[R] images with \f[B]boot\f[R], the
\f[B]ForeignUIDRange=\f[R] option has to be enabled when building the
image.
To use \f[B]disk\f[R] images with \f[B]boot\f[R], systemd\-nsresourced
and systemd\-mountfsd v260 or newer are required on the host.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]vm\f[R]
Similar to \f[B]boot\f[R], but uses the configured virtual machine
monitor (by default \f[B]qemu\f[R]) to boot up the image, i.e.\ instead
of container virtualization, virtual machine virtualization is used.
How extra command line arguments are interpreted depends on the
configured virtual machine monitor.
See \f[B]VirtualMachineMonitor=\f[R] for more information.
To pass extra options to the configured virtual machine monitor,
separate them from regular options with \f[B]\-\-\f[R].
.RS
To use \f[B]directory\f[R] images with \f[B]vm\f[R], the
\f[B]ForeignUIDRange=\f[R] option has to be enabled when building the
image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ssh\f[R]
When the image is built with the \f[B]Ssh=always\f[R] option or if
systemd\(cqs \f[B]sshd\-vsock\f[R] service is running in the VM (systemd
v256+), this command connects to a booted virtual machine via SSH.
Make sure to run \f[B]mkosi ssh\f[R] with the same config as \f[B]mkosi
build\f[R] so that it has the necessary information available to connect
to the running virtual machine via SSH.
Specifically, the SSH private key from the \f[B]SshKey=\f[R] setting is
used to connect to the virtual machine.
Use \f[B]mkosi genkey\f[R] to automatically generate a key and
certificate that will be picked up by \f[B]mkosi\f[R].
Any arguments passed after the \f[B]ssh\f[R] verb are passed as
arguments to the \f[B]ssh\f[R] invocation.
To pass extra options, separate them from regular options with
\f[B]\-\-\f[R].To connect to a container, use \f[B]machinectl login\f[R]
or \f[B]machinectl shell\f[R].
.RS
The \f[B]Machine=\f[R] option can be used to give the machine a custom
hostname when booting it which can later be used to \f[B]ssh\f[R] into
the image (e.g.\ \f[B]mkosi \-\-machine=mymachine vm\f[R] followed by
\f[B]mkosi \-\-machine=mymachine ssh\f[R]).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]journalctl\f[R]
Uses \f[B]journalctl\f[R] to inspect the journal inside the image.
All arguments specified after the \f[B]journalctl\f[R] verb and
separated by \f[B]\-\-\f[R] from the regular options are appended to the
\f[B]journalctl\f[R] invocation.
.TP
\f[B]coredumpctl\f[R]
Uses \f[B]coredumpctl\f[R] to look for coredumps inside the image.
All arguments specified after the \f[B]coredumpctl\f[R] verb and
separated by \f[B]\-\-\f[R] from the regular options are appended to the
\f[B]coredumpctl\f[R] invocation.
.TP
\f[B]sysupdate\f[R]
Invokes \f[B]systemd\-sysupdate\f[R] with the
\f[B]\-\-transfer\-source=\f[R] option set to the output directory and
the \f[B]\-\-definitions=\f[R] option set to the directory configured
with \f[B]SysupdateDirectory=\f[R].
All arguments specified after the \f[B]sysupdate\f[R] verb and separated
from the regular options with \f[B]\-\-\f[R] are passed directly to
\f[B]systemd\-sysupdate\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]box\f[R]
Run arbitrary commands inside of the same environment used to execute
other verbs such as \f[B]boot\f[R], \f[B]shell\f[R], \f[B]vm\f[R] and
more.
This means \f[B]/usr\f[R] will be replaced by \f[B]/usr\f[R] from the
tools tree if one is used while everything else will remain in place.
If no command is provided, \f[B]$SHELL\f[R] will be executed or
\f[B]bash\f[R] if \f[B]$SHELL\f[R] is not set.
To pass extra options to the given command, separate them from regular
options with \f[B]\-\-\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]clean\f[R]
Remove build artifacts generated on a previous build.
If combined with \f[B]\-f\f[R], also removes incremental build cache
images and the tools tree.
If \f[B]\-f\f[R] is specified twice, also removes any package cache.
.TP
\f[B]serve\f[R]
This builds the image if it is not built yet, and then serves the output
directory (i.e.\ usually \f[B]mkosi.output/\f[R], see below) via a small
embedded HTTP server, listening on port 8081.
Combine with \f[B]\-f\f[R] in order to rebuild the image unconditionally
before serving it.
This command is useful for testing network\-based acquisition of OS
images, for example via \f[B]machinectl pull\-raw \&...\f[R] and
\f[B]machinectl pull\-tar \&...\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]burn <device>\f[R]
This builds the image if it is not built yet, and then writes it to the
specified block device.
The partition contents are written as\-is, but the GPT partition table
is corrected to match sector and disk size of the specified medium.
.TP
\f[B]bump\f[R]
Bumps the image version from \f[B]mkosi.version\f[R] and writes the
resulting version string to \f[B]mkosi.version\f[R].
This is useful for implementing a simple versioning scheme: each time
this verb is called the version is bumped in preparation for the
subsequent build.
Note that \f[B]\-\-auto\-bump\f[R]/\f[B]\-B\f[R] may be used to
automatically bump the version as part of a build.
The new version is only written to \f[B]mkosi.version\f[R] if the build
succeeds in that case.
.RS
If \f[B]mkosi.bump\f[R] exists, it is invoked to generate the new
version to be used instead of using mkosi\(cqs own logic.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]genkey\f[R]
Generate a pair of SecureBoot keys for usage with the
\f[B]SecureBootKey=\f[R]/\f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-key=\f[R] and
\f[B]SecureBootCertificate=\f[R]/\f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-certificate=\f[R]
options.
.TP
\f[B]documentation\f[R]
Show \f[B]mkosi\f[R]\(cqs documentation.
If no argument is given, the \f[B]mkosi\f[R] man page is shown, but the
arguments \f[B]mkosi\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\-initrd\f[R], \f[B]initrd\f[R],
\f[B]mkosi\-sandbox\f[R], \f[B]sandbox\f[R], \f[B]mkosi.news\f[R] and
\f[B]news\f[R] are supported and respectively show the man pages for
\f[B]mkosi\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\-initrd\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\-sandbox\f[R] and
\f[B]mkosi\f[R]\(cqs NEWS file.
.RS
By default this verb will try several ways to output the documentation,
but a specific option can be chosen with the \f[B]\-\-doc\-format\f[R]
option.
Distro packagers are encouraged to add a file \f[B]mkosi.1\f[R] into the
\f[B]mkosi/resources\f[R] directory of the Python package, if it is
missing, as well as to install it in the appropriate search path for man
pages.
The man page can be generated from the markdown file
\f[B]mkosi/resources/man/mkosi.1.md\f[R] e.g via \f[B]pandoc \-t man \-s
\-o mkosi.1 mkosi.1.md\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]completion\f[R]
Generate shell completion for the shell given as argument and print it
to stdout.
The arguments \f[B]bash\f[R], \f[B]fish\f[R], and \f[B]zsh\f[R] are
understood.
.TP
\f[B]dependencies\f[R]
Output the list of packages required by \f[B]mkosi\f[R] to build and
boot images.
.RS
This list can be piped directly to a package manager to install the
packages.
For example, if the host system uses the \f[B]dnf\f[R] package manager,
the packages could be installed as follows:
.IP
.EX
mkosi dependencies \f[B]|\f[R] xargs \-d \(aq\(rsn\(aq dnf install
.EE
By default, only the dependencies required to build images with mkosi
are shown.
Extra tools tree profiles can be enabled to also output the packages
belonging to those profiles.
For example, running \f[B]mkosi dependencies \-\- \-\-profile
runtime\f[R] will also output the packages in the runtime profile on top
of the regular packages.
See the documentation for \f[B]ToolsTreeProfiles=\f[R] for a list of
available profiles.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]latest\-snapshot\f[R]
Output the latest available snapshot in the configured mirror.
.RS
This verb is useful to automatically bump snapshots every so often.
Note that this verb only outputs the latest snapshot.
It\(cqs up to the caller to ensure that the snapshot is written to the
intended configuration file.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]help\f[R]
This verb is equivalent to the \f[B]\-\-help\f[R] switch documented
below: it shows a brief usage explanation.
.SS Command\-Line\-Only Options
Those settings cannot be configured in the configuration files.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-force\f[R], \f[B]\-f\f[R]
Replace the output file if it already exists, when building an image.
By default when building an image and an output artifact already exists
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will refuse operation.
Specify this option once to delete all build artifacts from a previous
run before re\-building the image.
If incremental builds are enabled, specifying this option twice will
ensure the intermediary cache files are removed, too, before the
re\-build is initiated.
If a package cache is used (also see the \f[B]FILES\f[R] section below),
specifying this option thrice will ensure the package cache is removed
too, before the re\-build is initiated.
For the \f[B]clean\f[R] operation this option has a slightly different
effect: by default the verb will only remove build artifacts from a
previous run, when specified once the incremental cache files and the
tools tree are deleted too, and when specified twice the package cache
is also removed.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-directory=\f[R], \f[B]\-C\f[R]
Takes a path to a directory.
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] switches to this directory before doing anything.
Note that the various configuration files are searched for in this
directory, hence using this option is an effective way to build a
project located in a specific directory.
Defaults to the current working directory.
If the empty string is specified, all configuration in the current
working directory will be ignored.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-debug\f[R]
Enable additional debugging output.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-debug\-shell\f[R]
When executing a command in the image fails, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will start
an interactive shell in the image allowing further debugging.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-debug\-workspace\f[R]
When specified, the workspace directory will not be deleted and its
location will be logged when \f[B]mkosi\f[R] exits.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-debug\-sandbox\f[R]
Run \f[B]mkosi\-sandbox\f[R] with \f[B]strace\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-debug\-timing\f[R]
Log the duration of each build step.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Show package version.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-help\f[R], \f[B]\-h\f[R]
Show brief usage information.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-genkey\-common\-name=\f[R]
Common name to be used when generating keys via \f[B]mkosi\f[R]\(cqs
\f[B]genkey\f[R] command.
Defaults to \f[B]mkosi of %u\f[R], where \f[B]%u\f[R] expands to the
username of the user invoking \f[B]mkosi\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-genkey\-valid\-days=\f[R]
Number of days that the keys should remain valid when generating keys
via \f[B]mkosi\f[R]\(cqs \f[B]genkey\f[R] command.
Defaults to two years (730 days).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-auto\-bump=\f[R], \f[B]\-B\f[R]
If specified, the version is bumped and if the build succeeds, the
version is written to \f[B]mkosi.version\f[R] in a fashion equivalent to
the \f[B]bump\f[R] verb.
This is useful for simple, linear version management: each build in a
series will have a version number one higher then the previous one.
.RS
If \f[B]mkosi.bump\f[R] exists, it is invoked to generate the new
version to be used instead of using mkosi\(cqs own logic.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-doc\-format\f[R]
The format to show the documentation in.
Supports the values \f[B]markdown\f[R], \f[B]man\f[R], \f[B]pandoc\f[R],
\f[B]system\f[R] and \f[B]auto\f[R].
In the case of \f[B]markdown\f[R] the documentation is shown in the
original Markdown format.
\f[B]man\f[R] shows the documentation in man page format, if it is
available.
\f[B]pandoc\f[R] will generate the man page format on the fly, if
\f[B]pandoc\f[R] is available.
\f[B]system\f[R] will show the system\-wide man page for
\f[B]mkosi\f[R], which may or may not correspond to the version you are
using, depending on how you installed \f[B]mkosi\f[R].
\f[B]auto\f[R], which is the default, will try all methods in the order
\f[B]man\f[R], \f[B]pandoc\f[R], \f[B]markdown\f[R], \f[B]system\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-json\f[R]
Show the summary output as JSON\-SEQ.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-wipe\-build\-dir\f[R], \f[B]\-w\f[R]
Wipe the build directory if one is configured before building the image.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rerun\-build\-scripts\f[R], \f[B]\-R\f[R]
Rerun build scripts.
Requires the \f[B]Incremental=\f[R] option to be enabled and the image
to have been built once already.
If \f[B]History=\f[R] is enabled, the history from the previous build
will be reused and no new history will be written.
.SS Supported output formats
The following output formats are supported:
.IP \(bu 2
Raw \f[I]GPT\f[R] disk image, created using \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R]
(\f[I]disk\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Plain directory, containing the OS tree (\f[I]directory\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Tar archive (\f[I]tar\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
CPIO archive (\f[I]cpio\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Unified Kernel Image (\f[I]UKI\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
\&...
and much more.
See \f[B]Format=\f[R] documentation below.
.PP
The output format may also be set to \f[I]none\f[R] to have
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] produce no image at all.
This can be useful if you only want to use the image to produce another
output in the build scripts (e.g.\ build an RPM).
.PP
When a \f[I]GPT\f[R] disk image is created, repart partition definition
files may be placed in \f[B]mkosi.repart/\f[R] to configure the
generated disk image.
.PP
It is highly recommended to run \f[B]mkosi\f[R] on a file system that
supports reflinks such as XFS and btrfs and to keep all related
directories on the same file system.
This allows \f[B]mkosi\f[R] to create images very quickly by using
reflinks to perform copying via copy\-on\-write operations.
.SS Configuration Settings
The following settings can be set through configuration files (the
syntax with \f[B]SomeSetting=value\f[R]) and on the command line (the
syntax with \f[B]\-\-some\-setting=value\f[R]).
For some command line parameters, a single\-letter shortcut is also
allowed.
In the configuration files, the setting must be in the appropriate
section, so the settings are grouped by section below.
.PP
Configuration is parsed in the following order:
.IP \(bu 2
The command line arguments are parsed.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.local.conf\f[R] and \f[B]mkosi.local/\f[R] are parsed if they
exists (in that order).
This file and directory should be in \f[B].gitignore\f[R] (or
equivalent) and are intended for local configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
If an option has a corresponding default path, it is parsed if the
corresponding default path exists.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R] is parsed if it exists in the directory configured
with \f[B]\-\-directory=\f[R] or the current working directory if
\f[B]\-\-directory=\f[R] is not used.
If the specified directory does not contain a \f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R] or
\f[B]mkosi.tools.conf\f[R] and a \f[B]mkosi/mkosi.conf\f[R] or
\f[B]mkosi/mkosi.tools.conf\f[R] exists, the configuration will be
parsed from the \f[B]mkosi/\f[R] subdirectory of the specified directory
instead.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.conf.d/\f[R] is parsed in the same directory as
\f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R] if it exists.
Each directory and each file with the \f[B].conf\f[R] extension in
\f[B]mkosi.conf.d/\f[R] is parsed.
Any directory in \f[B]mkosi.conf.d\f[R] is parsed as if it were a
regular top level directory, except for \f[B]mkosi.images/\f[R],
\f[B]mkosi.tools.conf\f[R] and \f[B]mkosi.initrd.conf\f[R], which are
only picked up in the top level directory.
.IP \(bu 2
If any profiles are configured, their configuration is parsed from the
\f[B]mkosi.profiles/\f[R] directory.
.IP \(bu 2
Subimages are parsed from the \f[B]mkosi.images/\f[R] directory if it
exists.
.PP
Note that settings configured via the command line always override
settings configured via configuration files.
If the same setting is configured more than once via configuration
files, later assignments override earlier assignments except for
settings that take a collection of values.
Also, settings read from \f[B]mkosi.local.conf\f[R] or
\f[B]mkosi.local/\f[R] will override settings from configuration files
that are parsed later, but not settings specified on the CLI.
.PP
For settings that take a single value, the empty assignment
(\f[B]SomeSetting=\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-some\-setting=\f[R]) can be used to
override a previous setting and reset to the default.
.PP
Settings that take a collection of values are merged by appending the
new values to the previously configured values.
Assigning the empty string to such a setting removes all previously
assigned values, and overrides any configured default values as well.
The values specified on the CLI are appended after all the values from
configuration files.
.PP
To conditionally include configuration files, the \f[B][Match]\f[R]
section can be used.
A \f[B][Match]\f[R] section consists of individual conditions.
Conditions can use a pipe symbol (\f[B]|\f[R]) after the equals sign
(\f[B]\&...=|\&...\f[R]), which causes the condition to become a
triggering condition.
The config file will be included if the logical AND of all
non\-triggering conditions and the logical OR of all triggering
conditions is satisfied.
To negate the result of a condition, prefix the argument with an
exclamation mark.
If an argument is prefixed with the pipe symbol and an exclamation mark,
the pipe symbol must be passed first, and the exclamation second.
.PP
Note that \f[B][Match]\f[R] conditions compare against the current
values of specific settings, and do not take into account changes made
to the setting in configuration files that have not been parsed yet
(settings specified on the CLI are taken into account).
Also note that matching against a setting and then changing its value
afterwards in a different config file may lead to unexpected results.
.PP
The \f[B][Match]\f[R] section of a \f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R] file in a
directory applies to the entire directory.
If the conditions are not satisfied, the entire directory is skipped.
The \f[B][Match]\f[R] sections of files in \f[B]mkosi.conf.d/\f[R] and
\f[B]mkosi.local.conf\f[R] only apply to the file itself.
.PP
If there are multiple \f[B][Match]\f[R] sections in the same
configuration file, each of them has to be satisfied in order for the
configuration file to be included.
Specifically, triggering conditions only apply to the current
\f[B][Match]\f[R] section and are reset between multiple
\f[B][Match]\f[R] sections.
As an example, the following will only match if the output format is one
of \f[B]disk\f[R] or \f[B]directory\f[R] and the architecture is one of
\f[B]x86\-64\f[R] or \f[B]arm64\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Match]\f[R]
Format=|disk
Format=|directory

\f[B][Match]\f[R]
Architecture=|x86\-64
Architecture=|arm64
.EE
.PP
The \f[B][TriggerMatch]\f[R] section can be used to indicate triggering
matches.
These are identical to triggering conditions in systemd units except
they apply to the entire match section instead of just a single
condition.
As an example, the following will match if the distribution is
\f[B]debian\f[R] and the release is \f[B]bookworm\f[R] or if the
distribution is \f[B]ubuntu\f[R] and the release is \f[B]noble\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B][TriggerMatch]\f[R]
Distribution=debian
Release=bookworm

\f[B][TriggerMatch]\f[R]
Distribution=ubuntu
Release=noble
.EE
.PP
The semantics of conditions in \f[B][TriggerMatch]\f[R] sections is the
same as in \f[B][Match]\f[R], i.e.\ all normal conditions are joined by
a logical AND and all triggering conditions are joined by a logical OR.
When mixing \f[B][Match]\f[R] and \f[B][TriggerMatch]\f[R] sections, a
match is achieved when all \f[B][Match]\f[R] sections match and at least
one \f[B][TriggerMatch]\f[R] section matches.
The absence of match sections is valued as true.
Logically this means:
.IP
.EX
(⋀ᵢ Matchᵢ) ∧ (⋁ᵢ TriggerMatchᵢ)
.EE
.PP
There is also support for \f[B][Assert]\f[R] and
\f[B][TriggerAssert]\f[R] sections which behave identically to match
sections except parsing configuration will fail if the assert sections
are not satisfied, i.e.\ all \f[B][Assert]\f[R] sections in a file as
well as at least one \f[B][TriggertAssert]\f[R] section have to be
satisfied or config parsing will fail.
.PP
Command line options that take no argument are shown without \f[B]=\f[R]
in their long version.
In the config files, they should be specified with a boolean argument:
either \f[B]1\f[R], \f[B]yes\f[R], or \f[B]true\f[R] to enable, or
\f[B]0\f[R], \f[B]no\f[R], \f[B]false\f[R] to disable.
.SS [Distribution] Section
.TP
\f[B]Distribution=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-distribution=\f[R], \f[B]\-d\f[R]
The distribution to install in the image.
Takes one of the following arguments: \f[B]fedora\f[R],
\f[B]debian\f[R], \f[B]kali\f[R], \f[B]ubuntu\f[R], \f[B]arch\f[R],
\f[B]opensuse\f[R], \f[B]mageia\f[R], \f[B]centos\f[R], \f[B]rhel\f[R],
\f[B]rhel\-ubi\f[R], \f[B]openmandriva\f[R], \f[B]rocky\f[R],
\f[B]alma\f[R], \f[B]azure\f[R] or \f[B]custom\f[R].
If not specified, defaults to the distribution of the host or
\f[B]custom\f[R] if the distribution of the host is not a supported
distribution.
.TP
\f[B]Release=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-release=\f[R], \f[B]\-r\f[R]
The release of the distribution to install in the image.
The precise syntax of the argument this takes depends on the
distribution used, and is either a numeric string (in case of Fedora
Linux, CentOS, \&..., e.g.\ \f[B]29\f[R]), or a distribution version
name (in case of Debian, Kali, Ubuntu, \&..., e.g.\ \f[B]artful\f[R]).
Defaults to a recent version of the chosen distribution, or the version
of the distribution running on the host if it matches the configured
distribution.
.TP
\f[B]Architecture=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-architecture=\f[R]
The architecture to build the image for.
The architectures that are actually supported depends on the
distribution used and whether a bootable image is requested or not.
When building for a foreign architecture, you\(cqll also need to install
and register a user mode emulator for that architecture.
.RS
One of the following architectures can be specified per image built:
\f[B]alpha\f[R], \f[B]arc\f[R], \f[B]arm\f[R], \f[B]arm64\f[R],
\f[B]ia64\f[R], \f[B]loongarch64\f[R], \f[B]mips64\-le\f[R],
\f[B]mips\-le\f[R], \f[B]parisc\f[R], \f[B]ppc\f[R], \f[B]ppc64\f[R],
\f[B]ppc64\-le\f[R], \f[B]riscv32\f[R], \f[B]riscv64\f[R],
\f[B]s390\f[R], \f[B]s390x\f[R], \f[B]tilegx\f[R], \f[B]x86\f[R],
\f[B]x86\-64\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Mirror=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-mirror=\f[R], \f[B]\-m\f[R]
The mirror to use for downloading the distribution packages.
Expects a mirror URL as argument.
If not provided, the default mirror for the distribution is used.
.RS
The default mirrors for each distribution are as follows (unless
specified, the same mirror is used for all architectures):
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(13.3n) lw(30.0n) lw(26.7n).
