\f[B]fedora\f[R]
T}@T{
https://kojipkgs.fedoraproject.org
T}@T{
T}
.TE
For any distribution not listed above, snapshots are not supported.
Note that this setting does not apply to the default tools tree; use
\f[B]ToolsTreeSnapshot=\f[R] for that.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]LocalMirror=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-local\-mirror=\f[R]
The mirror will be used as a local, plain and direct mirror instead of
using it as a prefix for the full set of repositories normally supported
by distributions.
Useful for fully offline builds with a single repository.
Supported on \f[B]deb\f[R]\-, \f[B]rpm\f[R]\-, and
\f[B]pacman\f[R]\-based distributions.
Overrides \f[B]\-\-mirror=\f[R] but only for the local \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
build, it will not be configured inside the final image,
\f[B]\-\-mirror=\f[R] (or the default repository) will be configured
inside the final image instead.
.TP
\f[B]RepositoryKeyCheck=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-repository\-key\-check=\f[R]
Controls signature/key checks when using repositories, enabled by
default.
Useful to disable checks when combined with \f[B]\-\-local\-mirror=\f[R]
and using only a repository from a local filesystem.
.TP
\f[B]RepositoryKeyFetch=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-repository\-key\-fetch=\f[R]
Controls whether \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will fetch distribution GPG keys
remotely.
Enabled by default on Ubuntu when not using a tools tree or when using
Ubuntu tools trees to build Arch Linux or RPM\-based distributions.
Disabled by default on all other distributions.
When disabled, the distribution GPG keys for the target distribution
have to be installed locally on the host system alongside the package
manager for that distribution.
.RS
This setting is only implemented for distributions using \f[B]dnf\f[R],
\f[B]pacman\f[R] or \f[B]zypper\f[R] as their package manager.
For other distributions the distribution GPG keys are always looked up
locally regardless of the value of this setting.
To make the distribution GPG keys for distributions available without
enabling this setting, the corresponding package has to be installed on
the host.
This is usually one of \f[B]archlinux\-keyring\f[R],
\f[B]debian\-keyring\f[R], \f[B]kali\-archive\-keyring\f[R],
\f[B]ubuntu\-keyring\f[R] or \f[B]distribution\-gpg\-keys\f[R] (for
RPM\-based distributions).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Repositories=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-repositories=\f[R]
Enable package repositories that are disabled by default.
This can be used to enable the EPEL repos for CentOS or different
components of the Debian/Kali/Ubuntu repositories.
.SS [Output] Section
.TP
\f[B]Format=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-format=\f[R], \f[B]\-t\f[R]
The image format type to generate.
One of \f[B]directory\f[R] (for generating an OS image directly in a
local directory), \f[B]tar\f[R] (similar, but a tarball of the OS image
is generated), \f[B]cpio\f[R] (similar, but a cpio archive is
generated), \f[B]disk\f[R] (a block device OS image with a GPT partition
table), \f[B]uki\f[R] (a unified kernel image with the OS image in the
\f[B].initrd\f[R] PE section), \f[B]esp\f[R] (a disk image with only an
ESP partition, bootloader and optionally a UKI), \f[B]oci\f[R] (a
directory compatible with the OCI image specification),
\f[B]sysext\f[R], \f[B]confext\f[R], \f[B]portable\f[R], \f[B]addon\f[R]
or \f[B]none\f[R] (the OS image is solely intended as a build image to
produce another artifact).
.RS
If the \f[B]disk\f[R] output format is used, the disk image is generated
using \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R].
The repart partition definition files to use can be configured using the
\f[B]RepartDirectories=\f[R] setting or via \f[B]mkosi.repart/\f[R].
When verity partitions are configured using
\f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R]\(cqs \f[B]Verity=\f[R] setting,
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will automatically parse the verity hash partition\(cqs
roothash from \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R]\(cqs JSON output and include it
in the kernel command line of every unified kernel image built by
\f[B]mkosi\f[R].
If the \f[B]none\f[R] output format is used, the outputs from a previous
build are not removed, but clean scripts (see \f[B]CleanScripts=\f[R])
are still executed.
This allows rerunning a build script (see \f[B]BuildScripts=\f[R])
without removing the results of a previous build.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ManifestFormat=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-manifest\-format=\f[R]
The manifest format type or types to generate.
A comma\-delimited list consisting of \f[B]json\f[R] (the standard JSON
output format that describes the packages installed),
\f[B]changelog\f[R] (a human\-readable text format designed for
diffing).
By default no manifest is generated.
.TP
\f[B]Output=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-output=\f[R], \f[B]\-o\f[R]
Name to use for the generated output image file or directory.
Defaults to \f[B]image\f[R] or, if \f[B]ImageId=\f[R] is specified, it
is used as the default output name, optionally suffixed with the version
set with \f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R] or if a specific image is built from
\f[B]mkosi.images\f[R], the name of the image is preferred over
\f[B]ImageId\f[R].
Note that this option does not allow configuring the output directory,
use \f[B]OutputDirectory=\f[R] for that.
.RS
Note that this only specifies the output prefix, depending on the
specific output format, compression and image version used, the full
output name might be \f[B]image_7.8.raw.xz\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]OutputExtension=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-output\-extension=\f[R]
Use the specified extension for the output file.
Defaults to the appropriate extension based on the output format.
Only includes the file extension, not any compression extension which
will be appended to this extension if compression is enabled.
.TP
\f[B]OutputSize=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-output\-size=\f[R]
If set, the output disk image is resized to the given size.
Takes a size in bytes.
The suffixes \f[B]K\f[R], \f[B]M\f[R] and \f[B]G\f[R] can be used to
specify the size in kilobytes, megabytes and gigabytes.
This setting only grows the disk and is limited to full disk images and
no other output format.
.TP
\f[B]CompressOutput=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-compress\-output=\f[R]
Configure compression for the resulting image or archive.
The argument can be either a boolean or a compression algorithm
(\f[B]xz\f[R], \f[B]zstd\f[R]).
\f[B]zstd\f[R] compression is used by default, except CentOS and
derivatives up to version 8, which default to \f[B]xz\f[R], and OCI
images, which default to \f[B]gzip\f[R].
Note that when applied to block device image types, compression means
the image cannot be started directly but needs to be decompressed first.
This also means that the \f[B]shell\f[R], \f[B]boot\f[R], \f[B]vm\f[R]
verbs are not available when this option is used.
Implied for \f[B]tar\f[R], \f[B]cpio\f[R], \f[B]uki\f[R], \f[B]esp\f[R],
\f[B]oci\f[R] and \f[B]addon\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]CompressLevel=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-compress\-level=\f[R]
Configure the compression level to use.
Takes an integer.
The possible values depend on the compression being used.
.TP
\f[B]OutputDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-output\-directory=\f[R], \f[B]\-O\f[R]
Path to a directory where to place all generated artifacts.
If this is not specified and the directory \f[B]mkosi.output/\f[R]
exists in the local directory, it is automatically used for this
purpose.
.TP
\f[B]OutputMode=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-output\-mode=\f[R]
File system access mode used when creating the output image file.
Takes an access mode in octal notation.
If not set, uses the current system defaults.
.TP
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-image\-version=\f[R]
Configure the image version.
This accepts any string, but it is recommended to specify a series of
dot separated components.
The version may also be configured by reading a \f[B]mkosi.version\f[R]
file (in which case it may be conveniently managed via the
\f[B]bump\f[R] verb or the \f[B]\-\-auto\-bump\f[R] option) or by
reading stdout if it is executable (see the \f[B]Scripts\f[R] section
below).
When specified the image version is included in the default output file
name, i.e.\ instead of \f[B]image.raw\f[R] the default will be
\f[B]image_0.1.raw\f[R] for version \f[B]0.1\f[R] of the image, and
similar.
The version is also passed via the \f[B]$IMAGE_VERSION\f[R] to any build
scripts invoked (which may be useful to patch it into
\f[B]/usr/lib/os\-release\f[R] or similar, in particular the
\f[B]IMAGE_VERSION=\f[R] field of it).
.TP
\f[B]ImageId=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-image\-id=\f[R]
Configure the image identifier.
This accepts a freeform string that shall be used to identify the image
with.
If set the default output file will be named after it (possibly suffixed
with the version).
The identifier is also passed via the \f[B]$IMAGE_ID\f[R] to any build
scripts invoked.
The image ID is automatically added to \f[B]/usr/lib/os\-release\f[R].
When using \f[B]Format=oci\f[R], the image ID is also set as the
\f[B]org.opencontainers.image.ref.name\f[R] annotation on the OCI index
descriptor.
.TP
\f[B]OciLabels=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-oci\-labels=\f[R]
Set OCI config labels on images produced with \f[B]Format=oci\f[R].
Takes a space\-separated list of \f[B]KEY=VALUE\f[R] assignments.
These labels are stored in the OCI image config blob and are visible via
\f[B]podman inspect\f[R] or \f[B]docker inspect\f[R] (equivalent to
\f[B]LABEL\f[R] in a Containerfile).
This option may be specified more than once, in which case all listed
labels will be set.
If the same label is set twice, the later setting overrides the earlier
one.
.TP
\f[B]OciAnnotations=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-oci\-annotations=\f[R]
Set OCI manifest annotations on images produced with
\f[B]Format=oci\f[R].
Takes a space\-separated list of \f[B]KEY=VALUE\f[R] assignments.
These annotations are stored in the OCI image manifest.
mkosi automatically sets \f[B]io.systemd.mkosi.version\f[R] and, if
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R] is set,
\f[B]org.opencontainers.image.version\f[R].
User\-specified annotations override these defaults.
This option may be specified more than once, in which case all listed
annotations will be set.
.TP
\f[B]SplitArtifacts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-split\-artifacts=\f[R]
The artifact types to split out of the final image.
A comma\-delimited list consisting of \f[B]uki\f[R], \f[B]kernel\f[R],
\f[B]initrd\f[R], \f[B]os\-release\f[R], \f[B]pcrs\f[R],
\f[B]partitions\f[R], \f[B]roothash\f[R],
\f[B]kernel\-modules\-initrd\f[R], \f[B]repart\-definitions\f[R] and
\f[B]tar\f[R].
When building a bootable image \f[B]kernel\f[R] and \f[B]initrd\f[R]
correspond to their artifact found in the image (or in the UKI), while
\f[B]uki\f[R] copies out the entire UKI.
If \f[B]pcrs\f[R] is specified, a JSON file containing the
pre\-calculated TPM2 digests is written out, according to the \c
.UR https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/unified_kernel_image/#json-format-for-pcrsig
UKI specification
.UE \c
, which is useful for offline signing.
.RS
When building a disk image and \f[B]partitions\f[R] is specified, pass
\f[B]\-\-split=yes\f[R] to \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] to have it write
out split partition files for each configured partition.
Read the \c
.UR https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/systemd-repart.html#--split=BOOL
man
.UE \c
\ page for more information.
This is useful in A/B update scenarios where an existing disk image
shall be augmented with a new version of a root or \f[B]/usr\f[R]
partition along with its Verity partition and unified kernel.
When \f[B]tar\f[R] is specified, the rootfs is additionally archived as
a tar archive (compressed according to \f[B]CompressOutput=\f[R]).
When \f[B]roothash\f[R] is specified and a dm\-verity disk image is
built, the dm\-verity roothash is written out as a separate file, which
is useful for offline signing.
\f[B]kernel\-modules\-initrd\f[R] corresponds to the separate kernel
modules initrd which mkosi appends to the main initrd.
This is primarily intended for debugging as many initrd inspection tools
don\(cqt properly handle multiple initrds appended to each other.
When \f[B]repart\-definitions\f[R] is specified, a directory containing
the used repart definition files is written to the output directory.
If multiple directories are configured via \f[B]RepartDirectories=\f[R],
they are merged, with later directories taking priority over earlier
ones when files with identical names exist.
By default \f[B]uki\f[R], \f[B]kernel\f[R] and \f[B]initrd\f[R] are
split out.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RepartDirectories=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-repart\-directory=\f[R]
Paths to directories containing \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] partition
definition files that are used when \f[B]mkosi\f[R] invokes
\f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] when building a disk image.
If \f[B]mkosi.repart/\f[R] exists in the local directory, it will be
used for this purpose as well.
Note that \f[B]mkosi\f[R] invokes repart with \f[B]\-\-root=\f[R] set to
the root of the image root, so any \f[B]CopyFiles=\f[R] source paths in
partition definition files will be relative to the image root directory.
.TP
\f[B]SectorSize=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sector\-size=\f[R]
Override the default sector size that \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] uses
when building a disk image.
.TP
\f[B]Overlay=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-overlay=\f[R]
When used together with \f[B]BaseTrees=\f[R], the output will consist
only out of changes to the specified base trees.
Each base tree is attached as a lower layer in an overlayfs structure,
and the output becomes the upper layer, initially empty.
Thus files that are not modified compared to the base trees will not be
present in the final output.
.RS
This option may be used to create \c
.UR https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/extension_image
systemd \f[I]system extensions\f[R]
.UE \c
\&.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Seed=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-seed=\f[R]
Takes a UUID as argument or the special value \f[B]random\f[R].
Overrides the seed that \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] uses when building a
disk image.
This is useful to achieve reproducible builds, where deterministic UUIDs
and other partition metadata should be derived on each build.
If not specified explicitly and the file \f[B]mkosi.seed\f[R] exists in
the local directory, the UUID to use is read from it.
Otherwise, a random UUID is used.
.TP
\f[B]ElTorito=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-el\-torito=\f[R]
Whether to add an El Torito boot catalog to boot the ESP when building a
disk image.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
When set to \f[B]auto\f[R], an El Torito boot catalog is added unless
grub is installed as the BIOS bootloader (as that is incompatible).
Requires systemd\-repart ≥ v261.
.TP
\f[B]ElToritoSystem=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-el\-torito\-system=\f[R]
Set the system identifier in the ISO 9660 descriptor.
.TP
\f[B]ElToritoVolume=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-el\-torito\-volume=\f[R]
Set the volume identifier in the ISO 9660 descriptor.
.TP
\f[B]ElToritoPublisher=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-el\-torito\-publisher=\f[R]
Set the publisher identifier in the ISO 9660 descriptor.
.TP
\f[B]CleanScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-clean\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the clean scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.SS [Content] Section
.TP
\f[B]Packages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-package=\f[R], \f[B]\-p\f[R]
Install the specified distribution packages (i.e.\ RPM, deb, \&...)
in the image.
Takes a comma\-separated list of package specifications.
This option may be used multiple times in which case the specified
package lists are combined.
Use \f[B]BuildPackages=\f[R] to specify packages that shall only be
installed in an overlay that is mounted when the prepare scripts are
executed with the \f[B]build\f[R] argument and when the build scripts
are executed.
.RS
The types and syntax of \f[I]package specifications\f[R] that are
allowed depend on the package installer (e.g.\ \f[B]dnf\f[R] for
RPM\-based distros or \f[B]apt\f[R] for deb\-based distros), but may
include package names, package names with version and/or architecture,
package name globs, package groups, and virtual provides, including file
paths.
See \f[B]PackageDirectories=\f[R] for information on how to make local
packages available for installation with \f[B]Packages=\f[R].
\f[B]Example\f[R]: when using a distro that uses \f[B]dnf\f[R], the
following configuration would install the \f[B]meson\f[R] package (in
the latest version), the 32\-bit version of the
\f[B]libfdisk\-devel\f[R] package, all available packages that start
with the \f[B]git\-\f[R] prefix, a \f[B]systemd\f[R] RPM from the local
file system, one of the packages that provides \f[B]/usr/bin/ld\f[R],
the packages in the \f[I]Development Tools\f[R] group, and the package
that contains the \f[B]mypy\f[R] python module.
.IP
.EX
Packages=meson
         libfdisk\-devel.i686
         git\-*
         /usr/bin/ld
         \(atdevelopment\-tools
         python3dist(mypy)
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]BuildPackages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-build\-package=\f[R]
Similar to \f[B]Packages=\f[R], but configures packages to install only
in an overlay that is made available on top of the image to the prepare
scripts when executed with the \f[B]build\f[R] argument and the build
scripts.
This option should be used to list packages containing header files,
compilers, build systems, linkers and other build tools the
\f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] scripts require to operate.
Note that packages listed here will be absent in the final image.
.TP
\f[B]VolatilePackages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-volatile\-package=\f[R]
Similar to \f[B]Packages=\f[R], but packages configured with this
setting are not cached when \f[B]Incremental=\f[R] is enabled and are
installed after executing any build scripts.
.RS
Specifically, this setting can be used to install packages that change
often or which are built by a build script.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]PackageDirectories=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-package\-directory=\f[R]
Specify directories containing extra packages to be made available
during the build.
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will create a local repository containing all packages
in these directories and make it available when installing packages or
running scripts.
If the \f[B]mkosi.packages/\f[R] directory is found in the local
directory it is also used for this purpose.
.TP
\f[B]VolatilePackageDirectories=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-volatile\-package\-directory=\f[R]
Like \f[B]PackageDirectories=\f[R], but any changes to the packages in
these directories will not invalidate the cached images if
\f[B]Incremental=\f[R] is enabled.
.RS
Additionally, build scripts can add more packages to the local
repository by placing the built packages in \f[B]$PACKAGEDIR\f[R].
The packages placed in \f[B]$PACKAGEDIR\f[R] are shared between all
image builds and thus available for installation in all images using
\f[B]VolatilePackages=\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]WithRecommends=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-with\-recommends=\f[R]
Configures whether to install recommended or weak dependencies,
depending on how they are named by the used package manager, or not.
By default, recommended packages are not installed.
This is only used for package managers that support the concept, which
are currently \f[B]apt\f[R], \f[B]dnf\f[R] and \f[B]zypper\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]WithDocs=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-with\-docs=\f[R]
Include documentation in the image.
Enabled by default.
When disabled, if the underlying distribution package manager supports
it documentation is not included in the image.
The \f[B]$WITH_DOCS\f[R] environment variable passed to the
\f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] scripts is set to \f[B]0\f[R] or \f[B]1\f[R]
depending on whether this option is enabled or disabled.
.TP
\f[B]BaseTrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-base\-tree=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to use as base trees.
When used, these base trees are each copied into the OS tree and form
the base distribution instead of installing the distribution from
scratch.
Only extra packages are installed on top of the ones already installed
in the base trees.
Note that for this to work properly, the base image still needs to
contain the package manager metadata by setting
\f[B]CleanPackageMetadata=no\f[R] (see \f[B]CleanPackageMetadata=\f[R]).
.RS
Instead of a directory, a tar file or a disk image may be provided.
In this case it is unpacked into the OS tree.
This mode of operation allows setting permissions and file ownership
explicitly, in particular for projects stored in a version control
system such as \f[B]git\f[R] which retain full file ownership and access
mode metadata for committed files.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]SkeletonTrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-skeleton\-tree=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of colon\-separated path pairs.
The first path of each pair refers to a directory to copy into the OS
tree before invoking the package manager.
The second path of each pair refers to the target directory inside the
image.
If the second path is not provided, the directory is copied on top of
the root directory of the image.
The second path is always interpreted as an absolute path.
Use this to insert files and directories into the OS tree before the
package manager installs any packages.
If the \f[B]mkosi.skeleton/\f[R] directory is found in the local
directory it is also used for this purpose with the root directory as
target (also see the \f[B]FILES\f[R] section below).
.RS
Note that skeleton trees are cached and any changes to skeleton trees
after a cached image has been built (when using \f[B]Incremental=\f[R])
are only applied when the cached image is rebuilt (by using
\f[B]\-ff\f[R] or running \f[B]mkosi \-f clean\f[R]).
As with the base tree logic above, instead of a directory, a tar file
may be provided too.
\f[B]mkosi.skeleton.tar\f[R] will be automatically used if found in the
local directory.
To add extra package manager configuration files such as extra
repositories, use \f[B]SandboxTrees=\f[R] as \f[B]mkosi\f[R] invokes the
package managers from outside the image and not inside so any package
manager configuration files provided via \f[B]SkeletonTrees=\f[R]
won\(cqt take effect when \f[B]mkosi\f[R] invokes a package manager to
install packages.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ExtraTrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-extra\-tree=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of colon\-separated path pairs.
The first path of each pair refers to a directory to copy from the host
into the image.
The second path of each pair refers to the target directory inside the
image.
If the second path is not provided, the directory is copied on top of
the root directory of the image.
The second path is always interpreted as an absolute path.
Use this to override any default configuration files shipped with the
distribution.
If the \f[B]mkosi.extra/\f[R] directory is found in the local directory
it is also used for this purpose with the root directory as target (also
see the \f[B]FILES\f[R] section below).
.RS
As with the base tree logic above, instead of a directory, a tar file
may be provided too.
\f[B]mkosi.extra.tar\f[R] will be automatically used if found in the
local directory.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RemovePackages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-remove\-package=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of package specifications for removal, in
the same format as \f[B]Packages=\f[R].
The removal will be performed as one of the last steps.
This step is skipped if \f[B]CleanPackageMetadata=no\f[R] is used.
.TP
\f[B]RemoveFiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-remove\-files=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of globs.
Files in the image matching the globs will be purged at the end.
.TP
\f[B]CleanPackageMetadata=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-clean\-package\-metadata=\f[R]
Enable/disable removal of package manager databases and repository
metadata at the end of installation.
Can be specified as \f[B]true\f[R], \f[B]false\f[R], or \f[B]auto\f[R]
(the default).
With \f[B]auto\f[R], package manager databases and repository metadata
will be removed if the respective package manager executable is
\f[I]not\f[R] present at the end of the installation.
.TP
\f[B]SourceDateEpoch=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-source\-date\-epoch=\f[R]
Takes a timestamp in seconds since the UNIX epoch as argument.
File modification times of all files will be clamped to this value.
The variable is also propagated to \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] and scripts
executed by \f[B]mkosi\f[R].
If not set explicitly, \f[B]SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\f[R] from
\f[B]\-\-environment=\f[R] and from the host environment are tried in
that order.
This is useful to make builds reproducible.
See \c
.UR https://reproducible-builds.org/specs/source-date-epoch/
SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
.UE \c
\ for more information.
.TP
\f[B]SyncScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sync\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the sync scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]PrepareScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-prepare\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the prepare scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]BuildScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-build\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the build scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]PostInstallationScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-postinst\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the post\-installation scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]FinalizeScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-finalize\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the finalize scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]PostOutputScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-postoutput\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the post output scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]Bootable=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-bootable=\f[R]
Takes a boolean or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Enables or disables generation of a bootable image.
If enabled, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will install an EFI bootloader, and add an
ESP partition when the disk image output is used.
If the selected EFI bootloader (see \f[B]Bootloader=\f[R]) is not
installed or no kernel images can be found, the build will fail.
\f[B]auto\f[R] behaves as if the option was enabled, but the build
won\(cqt fail if either no kernel images or the selected EFI bootloader
can\(cqt be found.
If disabled, no bootloader will be installed even if found inside the
image, no unified kernel images will be generated and no ESP partition
will be added to the image if the disk output format is used.
.RS
If the \f[B]esp\f[R] output format is used, an ESP partition and
bootloader are always added, and the \f[B]Bootable=\f[R] option only
controls whether a UKI is added to the ESP partition or not.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Bootloader=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-bootloader=\f[R]
Takes one of \f[B]none\f[R], \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R], \f[B]uki\f[R],
\f[B]grub\f[R], \f[B]systemd\-boot\-signed\f[R], \f[B]uki\-signed\f[R]
or \f[B]grub\-signed\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R].
If set to \f[B]none\f[R], no EFI bootloader will be installed into the
image.
If set to \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R], \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] will be
installed and for each installed kernel, a UKI will be generated and
stored in \f[B]EFI/Linux\f[R] in the ESP.
If set to \f[B]uki\f[R], a single UKI will be generated for the latest
installed kernel (the one with the highest version) which is installed
to \f[B]EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI\f[R] in the ESP.
If set to \f[B]grub\f[R], for each installed kernel, a UKI will be
generated and stored in \f[B]EFI/Linux\f[R] in the ESP.
For each generated UKI, a menu entry is appended to the grub
configuration in \f[B]grub/grub.cfg\f[R] in the ESP which chainloads
into the UKI.
A shim grub.cfg is also written to \f[B]EFI/<distribution>/grub.cfg\f[R]
in the ESP which loads \f[B]grub/grub.cfg\f[R] in the ESP for
compatibility with signed versions of grub which load the grub
configuration from this location.
.RS
The \f[B]signed\f[R] variants will only install pre\-signed EFI binaries
shipped by the distribution.
Kernels need to be placed into the root filesystem (for example using
\f[B]ExtraTrees=\f[R]) under \f[B]/usr/lib/modules/$version\f[R], named
\f[B]vmlinux\f[R] or \f[B]vmlinuz\f[R].
The \f[B]$version\f[R] is as produced by Kbuild\(cqs
\f[B]kernelversion\f[R] make target.
Note: When using \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] or
\f[B]systemd\-boot\-signed\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] expects the
\f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] EFI binaries to be present in the image.
Depending on your distribution, these may be packaged separately.
For example, Debian\- based images will need
\f[B]systemd\-boot\-efi\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]BiosBootloader=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-bios\-bootloader=\f[R]
Takes one of \f[B]none\f[R] or \f[B]grub\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]none\f[R].
If set to \f[B]none\f[R], no BIOS bootloader will be installed.
If set to \f[B]grub\f[R], grub is installed as the BIOS boot loader if a
bootable image is requested with the \f[B]Bootable=\f[R] option.
If no repart partition definition files are configured, \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
will add a grub BIOS boot partition and an EFI system partition to the
default partition definition files.
.RS
Note that this option is not mutually exclusive with
\f[B]Bootloader=\f[R].
It is possible to have an image that is both bootable on UEFI and BIOS
by configuring both \f[B]Bootloader=\f[R] and \f[B]BiosBootloader=\f[R].
The grub BIOS boot partition should have UUID
\f[B]21686148\-6449\-6e6f\-744e\-656564454649\f[R] and should be at
least 1MB.
Even if no EFI bootloader is installed, we still need an ESP for BIOS
boot as that\(cqs where we store the kernel, initrd and grub modules.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ShimBootloader=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-shim\-bootloader=\f[R]
Takes one of \f[B]none\f[R], \f[B]unsigned\f[R], or \f[B]signed\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]none\f[R].
If set to \f[B]none\f[R], shim and MokManager will not be installed to
the ESP.
If set to \f[B]unsigned\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will search for unsigned
shim and MokManager EFI binaries and install them.
If \f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R] is enabled, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will sign the
unsigned EFI binaries before installing them.
If set to \f[B]signed\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will search for signed EFI
binaries and install those.
Even if \f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R] is enabled, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] won\(cqt sign
these binaries again.
.RS
Note that this option only takes effect when an image that is bootable
on UEFI firmware is requested using other options (\f[B]Bootable=\f[R],
\f[B]Bootloader=\f[R]).
Note that when this option is enabled, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will only install
already signed bootloader binaries, kernel image files and unified
kernel images as self\-signed binaries would not be accepted by the
signed version of shim.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]UnifiedKernelImages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-unified\-kernel\-images=\f[R]
Specifies whether to use unified kernel images or not when
\f[B]Bootloader=\f[R] is set to \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] or
\f[B]grub\f[R].
Takes one of \f[B]none\f[R], \f[B]unsigned\f[R], \f[B]signed\f[R] or
\f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
If \f[B]unsigned\f[R] or \f[B]signed\f[R], unified kernel images are
always used and the build will fail if any components required to build
unified kernel images are missing.
If set to \f[B]auto\f[R], unified kernel images will be used if all
necessary components are available.
Otherwise Type 1 entries as defined by the Boot Loader Specification
will be used instead.
If disabled, Type 1 entries will always be used.
If \f[B]Bootloader=\f[R] is set to one of the signed variant, a
pre\-built UKI will be searched and the build will fail if it cannot be
found, unless \f[B]UnifiedKernelImages=\f[R] is set to
\f[B]unsigned\f[R], in which case the UKI will be built locally.
This is useful when combined with the runtime \f[B]Firmware=\f[R] option
set to \f[B]custom\f[R] so that the local signing key is enrolled in
UEFI db.
.TP
\f[B]UnifiedKernelImageFormat=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-unified\-kernel\-image\-format=\f[R]
Takes a filename without any path components to specify the format that
unified kernel images should be installed as.
This may include both the regular specifiers (see \f[B]Specifiers\f[R])
and special delayed specifiers, that are expanded during the
installation of the files, which are described below.
The default format for this parameter is \f[B]&e\-&k\f[R] with
\f[B]\-&h\f[R] being appended if \f[B]roothash=\f[R] or
\f[B]usrhash=\f[R] is found on the kernel command line and \f[B]+&c\f[R]
if \f[B]/etc/kernel/tries\f[R] is found in the image.
.RS
The following specifiers may be used:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Specifier
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\f[B]&&\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]&\f[R] character
T}
T{
\f[B]&e\f[R]
T}@T{
Entry Token
T}
T{
\f[B]&k\f[R]
T}@T{
Kernel version
T}
T{
\f[B]&h\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]roothash=\f[R] or \f[B]usrhash=\f[R] value of kernel argument
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]UnifiedKernelImageProfiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-uki\-profile=\f[R]
Build additional UKI profiles.
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to UKI profile config files.
This option may be used multiple times in which case each config gets
built into a corresponding UKI profile.
Config files in the \f[B]mkosi.uki\-profiles/\f[R] directory are
automatically picked up.
All configured UKI profiles are added as additional UKI profiles to each
UKI built by \f[B]mkosi\f[R].
.RS
See the documentation for the \f[B]UKIProfile\f[R] section for
information on which settings can be configured in UKI profile config
files.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Initrds=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-initrd=\f[R]
Use user\-provided initrd(s).
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to initrd files.
This option may be used multiple times in which case the initrd lists
are combined.
If this setting is not specified and a bootable image is requested,
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will automatically build a default initrd if the image
looks like it might be a bootable image based on a few factors (output
format, is a kernel package installed, \&...).
To disable building the default initrd, assign the empty string to this
setting.
To build the default initrd even when specifying this setting, the
special value \f[B]default\f[R] can be assigned alongside the other
values.
.RS
When the default initrd is used, it can be further configured using the
settings below and with \f[B]mkosi.initrd.conf\f[R] which can either be
a file or directory containing extra configuration for the default
initrd.
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will also look for initrds in a subdirectory
\f[B]io.mkosi.initrd\f[R] of the artifact directory (see
\f[B]$ARTIFACTDIR\f[R] in the section \f[B]ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES\f[R]).
Any initrds found there are appended to the user\-provided initrd(s) and
any default initrd built by mkosi.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]InitrdProfiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-initrd\-profile=\f[R]
Set the profiles to enable for the default initrd.
Takes a comma\-delimited list of profiles.
By default, all profiles are disabled.
.RS
The \f[B]lvm\f[R] profile enables support for LVM.
The \f[B]network\f[R] profile enables support for network via
\f[B]systemd\-networkd\f[R].
The \f[B]nfs\f[R] profile enables support for NFS.
It requires networking in the initrd, using the \f[B]network\f[R]
profile, or some other custom method.
The \f[B]pkcs11\f[R] profile enables support for PKCS#11.
The \f[B]plymouth\f[R] profile provides a graphical interface at boot
(animation and password prompt).
The \f[B]raid\f[R] profile enables support for RAID arrays.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]InitrdPackages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-initrd\-package=\f[R]
Extra packages to install into the default initrd.
Takes a comma separated list of package specifications.
This option may be used multiple times in which case the specified
package lists are combined.
.TP
\f[B]InitrdVolatilePackages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-initrd\-volatile\-package=\f[R]
Similar to \f[B]VolatilePackages=\f[R], except it applies to the default
initrd.
.TP
\f[B]Devicetrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-devicetrees=\f[R]
Comma\-separated list of devicetree patterns for automatic
hardware\-based selection.
Patterns are glob expressions.
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] searches for devicetree files in standard locations
relative to \f[B]/usr/lib/modules/<kver>/dtb/\f[R],
\f[B]/usr/lib/firmware/<kver>/device\-tree/\f[R], and
\f[B]/usr/lib/linux\-image\-<kver>/\f[R].
.RS
For UKI builds, multiple matches enable automatic hardware\-based
selection using the \f[B].dtbauto\f[R] sections.
Type 1 boot entries require exactly one match.
Example: \f[B]Devicetrees=rockchip/*,imx.*\f[R] would include all
Rockchip devicetrees and any IMX devicetrees.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Splash=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-splash=\f[R]
When set, the boot splash for any unified kernel image built by
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will be picked up from the given path inside the image.
.TP
\f[B]MicrocodeHost=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-microcode\-host=\f[R]
When set to true only include microcode for the host\(cqs CPU in the
image.
.TP
\f[B]KernelCommandLine=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-kernel\-command\-line=\f[R]
Use the specified kernel command line when building images.
.RS
If the root or usr partition are created with verity enabled,
\f[B]roothash=\f[R] or \f[B]usrhash=\f[R] respectively are automatically
added to the kernel command line and \f[B]root=\f[R] or
\f[B]mount.usr=\f[R] should not be added.
Otherwise, if the value of this setting contains the literals
\f[B]root=PARTUUID\f[R] or \f[B]mount.usr=PARTUUID\f[R], these are
replaced with the partition UUID of the root or usr partition
respectively.
For example, \f[B]root=PARTUUID\f[R] would be replaced with
\f[B]root=PARTUUID=58c7d0b2\-d224\-4834\-a16f\-e036322e88f7\f[R] where
\f[B]58c7d0b2\-d224\-4834\-a16f\-e036322e88f7\f[R] is the partition UUID
of the root partition.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]KernelModules=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-kernel\-modules=\f[R]
Takes a list of glob patterns that specify which kernel modules to
include in the image.
Each argument may be prefixed with a dash (\f[B]\-\f[R]), to
\f[I]exclude\f[R] matching modules.
The arguments are evaluated in order, the last positive or negative
matching pattern determines the result.
The modules that were last matched by a positive pattern are included in
the image, as well as their module and firmware dependencies.
.RS
The \f[B].ko\f[R] suffix and compression suffix are ignored during
matching.
Globs are matched against module paths relative to
\f[B]/usr/lib/module/<kver>\f[R], e.g.\ the module at
\f[B]/usr/lib/module/<kver>/kernel/foo/bar.ko.xz\f[R] becomes
\f[B]kernel/foo/bar\f[R] for matching purposes.
Globs beginning with \(lq/\(rq are treated specially.
The glob is \f[I]first\f[R] matched against a path relative to
\f[B]/usr/lib/module/<kver>/kernel\f[R], and only then against
\f[B]/usr/lib/module/<kver>/\f[R].
This is a convenience, since usually only the in\-tree modules under
\f[B]kernel/\f[R] are of interest.
For example, the module
at\f[B]/usr/lib/module/<kver>/kernel/foo/bar.ko.xz\f[R] can be matched
by either \f[B]/foo/bar\f[R] or \f[B]/kernel/foo/bar\f[R].
The glob patterns may include just the basename (e.g.\ \f[B]loop\f[R]),
which must match the basename of the module, the relative path
(e.g.\ \f[B]block/loop\f[R]), which must match the final components of
the module path up to the basename, or an absolute path
(e.g.\ \f[B]/drivers/block/loop\f[R]), which must match the full path to
the module.
When suffixed with \f[B]/\f[R], the pattern will match all modules
underneath that directory.
The patterns may include shell\-style globs (\f[B]*\f[R], \f[B]?\f[R],
\f[B][\&...\-\&...]\f[R]).
If the special value \f[B]default\f[R] is used, the default kernel
modules defined in the \f[B]mkosi\-initrd\f[R] configuration are
included as well.
If the special value \f[B]host\f[R] is used, the currently loaded
modules on the host system are included as well.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]KernelModulesInitrd=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-kernel\-modules\-initrd=\f[R]
Boolean value, enabled (true) by default.
If enabled, when building a bootable image, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will
generate an extra initrd for each unified kernel image it assembles.
This initrd contains only modules and possibly firmware, and is then
appended to the base initrd to form the final initrd file.
This keeps the base initrd kernel independent, and only augments it with
the necessary kernel\-version specific modules when the UKI is
assembled.
.RS
If disabled, no extra initrd is generated.
Note that the kernel modules will still \f[B]not\f[R] be included in the
base initrd, which remains kernel independent.
Instead, it is assumed the user provides the necessary modules, if any,
via an additional custom initrd.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]KernelInitrdModules=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-kernel\-modules\-initrd\-include=\f[R]
Like \f[B]KernelModules=\f[R], but specifies the kernel modules to
include in the initrd.
.TP
\f[B]FirmwareFiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-firmware\-files=\f[R]
Takes a list of glob patterns that specify which firmware files to
include in the image.
The patterns are interpreted in the same way as in the
\f[B]KernelModules=\f[R] settings, except that the paths are relative to
\f[B]/usr/lib/firmware/<subdir>\f[R].
The compression suffix is ignored and must not be included in the
pattern.
.RS
Firmware dependencies of kernel modules installed in the image are
automatically included.
Example: \f[B]FirmwareFiles=cxgb4/bcm8483.bin\f[R] or
\f[B]FirmwareFiles=bcm8483.*\f[R] would both cause
\f[B]/usr/lib/firmware/cxgb4/bcm8483.bin.xz\f[R] to be included, even if
not listed by a module.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Locale=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-locale=\f[R], \f[B]LocaleMessages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-locale\-messages=\f[R], \f[B]Keymap=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-keymap=\f[R], \f[B]Timezone=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-timezone=\f[R], \f[B]Hostname=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-hostname=\f[R], \f[B]RootShell=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-root\-shell=\f[R]
The settings \f[B]Locale=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-locale=\f[R],
\f[B]LocaleMessages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-locale\-messages=\f[R],
\f[B]Keymap=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-keymap=\f[R], \f[B]Timezone=\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-timezone=\f[R], \f[B]Hostname=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-hostname=\f[R],
\f[B]RootShell=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-root\-shell=\f[R] correspond to the
identically named systemd\-firstboot options.
See \f[B]systemd\-firstboot\f[R](1) for more information.
Additionally, where applicable, the corresponding systemd credentials
for these settings are written to \f[B]/usr/lib/credstore\f[R], so that
they apply even if only \f[B]/usr\f[R] is shipped in the image.
.TP
\f[B]RootPassword=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-root\-password=\f[R],
Set the system root password.
If this option is not used, but a \f[B]mkosi.rootpw\f[R] file is found
in the local directory, the password is automatically read from it or if
the file is executable it is run as a script and stdout is read instead
(see the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section below).
If the password starts with \f[B]hashed:\f[R], it is treated as an
already hashed root password.
The root password is also stored in \f[B]/usr/lib/credstore\f[R] under
the appropriate systemd credential so that it applies even if only
\f[B]/usr\f[R] is shipped in the image.
To create an unlocked account without any password use \f[B]hashed:\f[R]
without a hash.
.TP
\f[B]Autologin=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-autologin=\f[R], \f[B]\-a\f[R]
Enable autologin for the \f[B]root\f[R] user on \f[B]/dev/pts/0\f[R]
(nspawn), \f[B]/dev/tty1\f[R] and \f[B]/dev/hvc0\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]MakeInitrd=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-make\-initrd=\f[R]
Add \f[B]/etc/initrd\-release\f[R] and \f[B]/init\f[R] to the image so
that it can be used as an initramfs.
.TP
\f[B]Ssh=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-ssh=\f[R]
Specifies whether to install an \f[B]sshd\f[R] socket unit and matching
service in the final image.
Takes one of \f[B]always\f[R], \f[B]never\f[R], \f[B]auto\f[R] or
\f[B]runtime\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
.RS
If set to \f[B]auto\f[R] and \f[B]sshd\f[R] is present in the image and
the generator binary \f[B]systemd\-ssh\-generator\f[R] is not present,
or if set to \f[B]always\f[R], mkosi will install \f[B]sshd\f[R] units
in the final image that expose SSH over VSock.
If set to \f[B]never\f[R], mkosi will not install these units.
If the \f[B]runtime\f[R] value is used, mkosi will also not install any
units but abort starting \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R] if no SSH credentials are
configured.
When building with this option and running the image using \f[B]mkosi
vm\f[R], the \f[B]mkosi ssh\f[R] command can be used to connect to the
container/VM via SSH.
Note that you still have to make sure openssh is installed in the image
to make \f[B]mkosi ssh\f[R] behave correctly.
Run \f[B]mkosi genkey\f[R] to automatically generate an X.509
certificate and private key to be used by \f[B]mkosi\f[R] to enable SSH
access to any virtual machines via \f[B]mkosi ssh\f[R].
To access images booted using \f[B]mkosi boot\f[R], use
\f[B]machinectl\f[R].
Starting with systemd v256, systemd\-ssh\-generator(8) will
automatically provide \f[B]sshd\f[R] over VSock when running inside a
VM.
So if you\(cqre using a recent version of systemd inside a VM, this
option is generally not used.
You still need openssh installed in the image, and the default setting
of \f[B]\-\-vsock=auto\f[R] is enough to ensure a VSock is available
inside the VM.
Note: if the image distro uses SELinux, mkosi\(cqs sshd service will be
denied access to the VSock, resulting in failure to connect to it from
the host.
You will need to either disable SELinux enforcement, or create a custom
policy module (e.g.\ with \f[B]audit2allow\f[R]).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]SELinuxRelabel=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-selinux\-relabel=\f[R]
Specifies whether to relabel files to match the image\(cqs SELinux
policy.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
If disabled, files will not relabeled.
If enabled, an SELinux policy has to be installed in the image and
\f[B]setfiles\f[R] has to be available to relabel files.
If any errors occur during \f[B]setfiles\f[R], the build will fail.
If set to \f[B]auto\f[R], files will be relabeled if mkosi is not
building a directory image, an SELinux policy is installed in the image
and if \f[B]setfiles\f[R] is available.
Any errors occurred during \f[B]setfiles\f[R] will be ignored.
.RS
Note that when running unprivileged, \f[B]setfiles\f[R] will fail to set
any labels that are not in the host\(cqs SELinux policy.
To ensure \f[B]setfiles\f[R] succeeds without errors, make sure to run
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] as root or build from a host system with the same
SELinux policy as the image you\(cqre building.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]MachineId=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-machine\-id=\f[R]
Takes a UUID or the special value \f[B]random\f[R].
Sets the machine ID of the image to the specified UUID.
If set to \f[B]random\f[R], a random UUID will be written to
\f[B]/etc/machine\-id\f[R].
If not specified explicitly and the file \f[B]mkosi.machine\-id\f[R]
exists in the local directory, the UUID to use is read from it.
Otherwise, \f[B]uninitialized\f[R] will be written to
\f[B]/etc/machine\-id\f[R].
.SS [Validation] Section
.TP
\f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot=\f[R]
Sign \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] (if it is not signed yet) and any generated
unified kernel images for UEFI SecureBoot.
.TP
\f[B]SecureBootAutoEnroll=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-auto\-enroll=\f[R]
Set up automatic enrollment of the secure boot keys in virtual machines
as documented in \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R](7) if \f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R] is
used.
Note that \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] will only do automatic secure boot key
enrollment in virtual machines starting from systemd v253.
To do auto enrollment on systemd v252 or on bare metal machines, write a
\f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] configuration file to
\f[B]/efi/loader/loader.conf\f[R] using an extra tree with
\f[B]secure\-boot\-enroll force\f[R] or \f[B]secure\-boot\-enroll
manual\f[R] in it.
Auto enrollment is not supported on systemd versions older than v252.
Defaults to \f[B]yes\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SecureBootKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-key=\f[R]
Path to the PEM file containing the secret key for signing the UEFI
kernel image if \f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R] is used and PCR signatures when
\f[B]SignExpectedPcr=\f[R] is also used.
When \f[B]SecureBootKeySource=\f[R] is specified, the input type depends
on the source.
.TP
\f[B]SecureBootCertificate=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-certificate=\f[R]
Path to the X.509 file containing the certificate for the signed UEFI
kernel image, if \f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R] is used.
.TP
\f[B]SecureBootSignTool=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-sign\-tool=\f[R]
Tool to use to sign secure boot PE binaries.
Takes one of \f[B]systemd\-sbsign\f[R], \f[B]sbsign\f[R] or
\f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
If set to \f[B]auto\f[R], either \f[B]systemd\-sbsign\f[R] or
\f[B]sbsign\f[R] are used if available, with \f[B]systemd\-sbsign\f[R]
being preferred.
.TP
\f[B]Verity=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verity=\f[R]
Whether to enforce or disable verity for extension images.
Takes one of \f[B]signed\f[R], \f[B]hash\f[R], \f[B]defer\f[R],
\f[B]auto\f[R] or a boolean value.
If set to \f[B]signed\f[R], a verity key and certificate must be present
and the build will fail if we don\(cqt detect any verity partitions in
the disk image produced by \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R].
If disabled, verity partitions will be excluded from the extension
images produced by \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R].
If set to \f[B]hash\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] configures
\f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] to create a verity hash partition, but no
signature partition.
If set to \f[B]defer\f[R], space for the verity sig partition will be
allocated but it will not be populated yet.
If set to \f[B]auto\f[R] and a verity key and certificate are present,
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will pass them to \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] and expects
the generated disk image to contain verity partitions, but the build
won\(cqt fail if no verity partitions are found in the disk image
produced by \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R].
.RS
Note that explicitly disabling verity signature and/or hash is not yet
implemented for the \f[B]disk\f[R] output and only works for extension
images at the moment.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]VerityKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verity\-key=\f[R]
Path to the PEM file containing the secret key for signing the verity
signature, if a verity signature partition is added with
\f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R].
When \f[B]VerityKeySource=\f[R] is specified, the input type depends on
the source.
.TP
\f[B]VerityCertificate=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verity\-certificate=\f[R]
Path to the X.509 file containing the certificate for signing the verity
signature, if a verity signature partition is added with
\f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SignExpectedPcr=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign\-expected\-pcr=\f[R]
Measure the components of the unified kernel image (UKI) using
\f[B]systemd\-measure\f[R] and embed the PCR signature into the unified
kernel image.
This option takes a boolean value or the special value \f[B]auto\f[R],
which is the default, which is equal to a true value if the
\f[B]systemd\-measure\f[R] binary is in \f[B]PATH\f[R].
Depends on \f[B]SecureBoot=\f[R] being enabled and key from
\f[B]SecureBootKey=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign\-expected\-pcr\-key=\f[R]
Path to the PEM file containing the secret key for signing the expected
PCR signatures.
When \f[B]SignExpectedPcrKeySource=\f[R] is specified, the input type
depends on the source.
.TP
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrCertificate=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign\-expected\-pcr\-certificate=\f[R]
Path to the X.509 file containing the certificate for signing the
expected PCR signatures.
.TP
\f[B]SignInitrdPCRs=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign\-initrd\-pcrs=\f[R]
Whether to generate signed PCR policies that can only be satisfied from
the initrd.
This is required for initialization of NvPCRs.
This takes a boolean value or the special value \f[B]auto\f[R], which is
the default and is equivalent to a true value if PCR signing is enabled
(see \f[B]SignExpectedPcr=\f[R]) and the version of \f[B]ukify\f[R] is
at least v262.
Signing is performed with the key that is supplied to
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrKey=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SecureBootKeySource=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-key\-source=\f[R], \f[B]VerityKeySource=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verity\-key\-source=\f[R], \f[B]SignExpectedPcrKeySource=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign\-expected\-key\-source=\f[R]
The source of the corresponding private key, to support OpenSSL engines
and providers,
e.g.\ \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-key\-source=engine:pkcs11\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-key\-source=provider:pkcs11\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SecureBootCertificateSource=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-certificate\-source=\f[R], \f[B]VerityCertificateSource=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verity\-certificate\-source=\f[R], \f[B]SignExpectedPcrCertificateSource=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign\-expected\-certificate\-source=\f[R]
The source of the corresponding certificate, to support OpenSSL
providers,
e.g.\ \f[B]\-\-secure\-boot\-certificate\-source=provider:pkcs11\f[R].
Note that engines are not supported.
.TP
\f[B]Passphrase=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-passphrase=\f[R]
Specify the path to a file containing the passphrase to use for LUKS
encryption.
It should contain the passphrase literally, and not end in a newline
character (i.e.\ in the same format as \f[B]cryptsetup\f[R] and
\f[B]/etc/crypttab\f[R] expect the passphrase files).
The file must have an access mode of 0600 or less.
.RS
Note that this setting on its own does not enable any encryption.
You also have to add one or more partition definition files to
\f[B]mkosi.repart/\f[R] with \f[B]Encrypt=key\-file\f[R] to add
encrypted partitions to the image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Checksum=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-checksum=\f[R]
Generate a \f[B]<output>.SHA256SUMS\f[R] file of all generated artifacts
after the build is complete.
.TP
\f[B]Sign=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sign=\f[R]
Sign the generated \f[B]SHA256SUMS\f[R] using \f[B]gpg\f[R] after
completion.
.TP
\f[B]OpenPGPTool=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-openpgp\-tool=\f[R]
OpenPGP implementation to use for signing.
\f[B]gpg\f[R] is the default.
Selecting a value different than the default will use the given
Stateless OpenPGP (SOP) tool for signing the \f[B]SHA256SUMS\f[R] file.
.RS
Exemplary choices are \f[B]sqop\f[R] and \f[B]rsop\f[R], but any
implementation from https://www.openpgp.org/about/sop/ that can be
installed locally will work.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Key=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-key=\f[R]
Select the \f[B]gpg\f[R] key to use for signing \f[B]SHA256SUMS\f[R].
This key must be already present in the \f[B]gpg\f[R] keyring.
.SS [Build] Section
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTree=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree=\f[R]
If specified, programs executed by \f[B]mkosi\f[R] to build and boot an
image are looked up inside the given tree instead of in the host system.
Use this option to make image builds more reproducible by always using
the same versions of programs to build the final image instead of
whatever version is installed on the host system.
If this option is not used, but the \f[B]mkosi.tools/\f[R] directory is
found in the local directory it is automatically used for this purpose
with the root directory as target.
.RS
The tools tree directory is kept between repeated image builds unless
cleaned by calling \f[B]mkosi clean \-f\f[R].
Note that binaries found in any of the paths configured with
\f[B]ExtraSearchPaths=\f[R] will be executed with \f[B]/usr/\f[R] from
the tools tree instead of from the host.
If the host distribution or release does not match the tools tree
distribution or release respectively, this might result in failures when
trying to execute binaries from any of the extra search paths.
If set to \f[B]yes\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will automatically add an extra
tools tree image and use it as the tools tree.
This image can be further configured using the settings below and with
\f[B]mkosi.tools.conf\f[R] which can either be a file or directory
containing extra configuration for the default tools tree.
See the \f[B]TOOLS TREE\f[R] section for further details.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeDistribution=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-distribution=\f[R]
Set the distribution to use for the default tools tree.
Defaults to the host distribution except if the host (not target)
distribution is Ubuntu, which defaults to Debian, and RHEL, CentOS, Alma
and Rocky, which default to Fedora, or \f[B]custom\f[R] if the
distribution of the host is not a supported distribution.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeRelease=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-release=\f[R]
Set the distribution release to use for the default tools tree.
By default, the hardcoded default release in \f[B]mkosi\f[R] for the
distribution is used.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeProfiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-profile=\f[R]
Set the profiles to enable for the default tools tree.
Takes a comma\-delimited list consisting of \f[B]devel\f[R],
\f[B]misc\f[R], \f[B]package\-manager\f[R] and \f[B]runtime\f[R].
By default, all profiles except \f[B]devel\f[R] and \f[B]gui\f[R] are
enabled.
.RS
The \f[B]devel\f[R] profile contains tools required to build (C/C++)
projects.
The \f[B]misc\f[R] profile contains various useful tools that are handy
to have available in scripts.
The package manager profile contains package managers and related tools
other than those native to the tools tree distribution.
The \f[B]runtime\f[R] profile contains the tools required to boot images
in a systemd\-nspawn container or in a virtual machine.
The \f[B]gui\f[R] profile contains tools required to boot images in a
virtual machine with audio and graphics support.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeMirror=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-mirror=\f[R]
Set the mirror to use for the default tools tree.
By default, the default mirror for the tools tree distribution is used.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeSnapshot=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-snapshot=\f[R]
Same as \f[B]Snapshot=\f[R] but for the default tools tree.
By default, if the tools tree distribution matches the image\(cqs
distribution, the image\(cqs snapshot is used.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeRepositories=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-repository=\f[R]
Same as \f[B]Repositories=\f[R] but for the default tools tree.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeSandboxTrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-sandbox\-tree=\f[R]
Same as \f[B]SandboxTrees=\f[R] but for the default tools tree.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreePackages=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-package=\f[R]
Extra packages to install into the default tools tree.
Takes a comma separated list of package specifications.
This option may be used multiple times in which case the specified
package lists are combined.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreePackageDirectories=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-package\-directory=\f[R]
Same as \f[B]PackageDirectories=\f[R], but for the default tools tree.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeCertificates=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tools\-tree\-certificates=\f[R]
Specify whether to use certificates and keys from the tools tree.
Enabled by default.
If enabled, \f[B]/etc/pki/ca\-trust\f[R], \f[B]/etc/pki/tls\f[R],
\f[B]/etc/ssl\f[R], \f[B]/etc/ca\-certificates\f[R], and
\f[B]/var/lib/ca\-certificates\f[R] from the tools tree are used.
Otherwise, these directories are picked up from the host.
.TP
\f[B]ExtraSearchPaths=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-extra\-search\-path=\f[R]
List of colon\-separated paths to look for tools in, before using the
regular \f[B]$PATH\f[R] search path.
.TP
\f[B]Incremental=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-incremental=\f[R], \f[B]\-i\f[R]
Takes either \f[B]strict\f[R] or a boolean value as its argument.
Enables incremental build mode.
In this mode, a copy of the OS image is created immediately after all OS
packages are installed and the prepare scripts have executed but before
the \f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] scripts are invoked (or anything that happens
after it).
On subsequent invocations of \f[B]mkosi\f[R] with the \f[B]\-i\f[R]
switch this cached image may be used to skip the OS package
installation, thus drastically speeding up repetitive build times.
Note that while there is some rudimentary cache invalidation, it is
definitely not perfect.
In order to force a rebuild of the cached image, combine \f[B]\-i\f[R]
with \f[B]\-ff\f[R] to ensure the cached image is first removed and then
re\-created.
.RS
If set to \f[B]strict\f[R], the build fails if previously built cached
image does not exist.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]CacheOnly=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-cache\-only=\f[R]
Takes one of \f[B]auto\f[R], \f[B]metadata\f[R], \f[B]always\f[R] or
\f[B]never\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
If \f[B]always\f[R], the package manager is instructed not to contact
the network.
This provides a minimal level of reproducibility, as long as the package
cache is already fully populated.
If set to \f[B]metadata\f[R], the package manager can still download
packages, but we won\(cqt sync the repository metadata.
If set to \f[B]auto\f[R], the repository metadata is synced when no
image has ever been built before, or (and all cached images are
invalidated in this case) when every image was previously cached (see
\f[B]Incremental=\f[R]) and at least one of those caches is now out of
date.
Packages can be downloaded during the build.
If set to \f[B]never\f[R], repository metadata is always synced and
packages can be downloaded during the build.
.TP
\f[B]SandboxTrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sandbox\-tree=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of colon\-separated path pairs.
The first path of each pair refers to a directory to copy into the mkosi
sandbox before executing a tool.
The second path of each pair refers to the target directory inside the
sandbox.
If the second path is not provided, the directory is copied on top of
the root directory of the sandbox.
The second path is always interpreted as an absolute path.
If the \f[B]mkosi.sandbox/\f[R] directory is found in the local
directory it is used for this purpose with the root directory as target
(also see the \f[B]FILES\f[R] section below).
.RS
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will look for the package manager configuration and
related files in the configured sandbox trees.
Unless specified otherwise, it will use the configuration files from
their canonical locations in \f[B]/usr\f[R] or \f[B]/etc\f[R] in the
sandbox trees.
For example, it will look for \f[B]/etc/dnf/dnf.conf\f[R] in the sandbox
trees if \f[B]dnf\f[R] is used to install packages.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]WorkspaceDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-workspace\-directory=\f[R]
Path to a directory where to store data required temporarily while
building the image.
This directory should have enough space to store the full OS image,
though in most modes the actually used disk space is smaller.
If not specified, a subdirectory of \f[B]$XDG_CACHE_HOME\f[R] (if set),
\f[B]$CACHE_DIRECTORY\f[R] (if set), \f[B]$HOME/.cache\f[R] (if set) or
\f[B]/var/tmp\f[R] is used.
.RS
The data in this directory is removed automatically after each build.
It\(cqs safe to manually remove the contents of this directory should an
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] invocation be aborted abnormally (for example, due to
reboot/power failure).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]CacheDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-cache\-directory=\f[R]
Takes a path to a directory to use as the incremental cache directory
for the incremental images produced when the \f[B]Incremental=\f[R]
option is enabled.
If this option is not used, but a \f[B]mkosi.cache/\f[R] directory is
found in the local directory it is automatically used for this purpose.
.TP
\f[B]CacheKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-cache\-key=\f[R]
Specifies the subdirectory within the cache directory where to store the
cached image.
This may include both the regular specifiers (see \f[B]Specifiers\f[R])
and special delayed specifiers, that are expanded after config parsing
has finished, instead of during config parsing, which are described
below.
The default format for this parameter is \f[B]&d\(ti&r\(ti&a\(ti&I\f[R].
.RS
The following specifiers may be used:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Specifier
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\f[B]&&\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]&\f[R] character
T}
T{
\f[B]&d\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]Distribution=\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]&r\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]Release=\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]&a\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]Architecture=\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]&i\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]ImageId=\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]&v\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]&I\f[R]
T}@T{
Subimage name within mkosi.images/ or \f[B]main\f[R]
T}
.TE
Note that all images within a build must have a unique cache key.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]PackageCacheDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-package\-cache\-dir=\f[R]
Takes a path to a directory to use as the package cache directory for
the distribution package manager used.
If unset, but a \f[B]mkosi.pkgcache/\f[R] directory is found in the
local directory it is automatically used for this purpose, otherwise a
suitable directory in the user\(cqs home directory or system is used.
.TP
\f[B]BuildDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-build\-directory=\f[R]
Takes a path to a directory to use as the build directory for build
systems that support out\-of\-tree builds (such as Meson).
The directory used this way is shared between repeated builds, and
allows the build system to reuse artifacts (such as object files,
executable, \&...)
generated on previous invocations.
The build scripts can find the path to this directory in the
\f[B]$BUILDDIR\f[R] environment variable.
This directory is mounted into the image\(cqs root directory when
\f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R] is invoked during execution of the build
scripts.
If this option is not specified, but a directory
\f[B]mkosi.builddir/\f[R] exists in the local directory it is
automatically used for this purpose (also see the \f[B]FILES\f[R]
section below).
.TP
\f[B]BuildKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-build\-key=\f[R]
Specifies the subdirectory within the build directory where to store
incremental build artifacts.
This may include both the regular specifiers (see \f[B]Specifiers\f[R])
and special delayed specifiers, that are expanded after config parsing
has finished, instead of during config parsing, which are the same
delayed specifiers that are supported by \f[B]CacheKey=\f[R].
The default format for this parameter is \f[B]&d\(ti&r\(ti&a\f[R].
.RS
To disable usage of a build subdirectory completely, assign a literal
\f[B]\-\f[R] to this setting.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]UseSubvolumes=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-use\-subvolumes=\f[R]
Takes a boolean or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Enables or disables use of btrfs subvolumes for directory tree outputs.
If enabled, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will create the root directory as a btrfs
subvolume and use btrfs subvolume snapshots where possible to copy base
or cached trees which is much faster than doing a recursive copy.
If explicitly enabled and \f[B]btrfs\f[R] is not installed or subvolumes
cannot be created, an error is raised.
If \f[B]auto\f[R], missing \f[B]btrfs\f[R] or failures to create
subvolumes are ignored.
.TP
\f[B]RepartOffline=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-repart\-offline=\f[R]
Specifies whether to build disk images using loopback devices.
Enabled by default.
When enabled, \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] will not use loopback devices to
build disk images.
When disabled, \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] will always use loopback
devices to build disk images.
.RS
Note that when using \f[B]RepartOffline=no\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] cannot
run unprivileged and the image build has to be done as the root user
outside of any containers and with loopback devices available on the
host system.
There are currently two known scenarios where \f[B]RepartOffline=no\f[R]
has to be used.
The first is when using \f[B]Subvolumes=\f[R] in a repart partition
definition file, as subvolumes cannot be created without using loopback
devices.
The second is when creating a system with SELinux and an XFS root
partition.
Because \f[B]mkfs.xfs\f[R] does not support populating an XFS filesystem
with extended attributes, loopback devices have to be used to ensure the
SELinux extended attributes end up in the generated XFS filesystem.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]History=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-history=\f[R]
Takes a boolean.
If enabled, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will write the configuration provided via
the CLI for the latest build to the \f[B].mkosi\-private\f[R]
subdirectory in the directory from which it was invoked.
These arguments are then reused as long as the image is not rebuilt to
avoid having to specify them over and over again.
.RS
To give an example of why this is useful, if you run \f[B]mkosi \-O
my\-custom\-output\-dir \-f\f[R] followed by \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R],
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will fail saying the image hasn\(cqt been built yet.
If you run \f[B]mkosi \-O my\-custom\-output\-dir \-\-history=yes
\-f\f[R] followed by \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R], it will boot the image built in
the previous step as expected.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]BuildSources=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-build\-sources=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of colon\-separated path pairs.
The first path of each pair refers to a directory to mount from the
host.
The second path of each pair refers to the directory where the source
directory should be mounted when running scripts.
Every target path is prefixed with \f[B]/work/src\f[R] and all build
sources are sorted lexicographically by their target before mounting, so
that top level paths are mounted first.
If not configured explicitly, the current working directory is mounted
to \f[B]/work/src\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]BuildSourcesEphemeral=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-build\-sources\-ephemeral=\f[R]
Takes a boolean or the special value \f[B]buildcache\f[R].
Disabled by default.
Configures whether changes to source directories, the working directory
and configured using \f[B]BuildSources=\f[R], are persisted.
If enabled, all source directories will be reset to their original state
every time after running all scripts of a specific type (except sync
scripts).
.RS
💥💣💥 If set to \f[B]buildcache\f[R] the overlay is not discarded when
running build scripts, but saved to the build directory, configured via
\f[B]BuildDirectory=\f[R], and will be reused on subsequent runs.
The overlay is still discarded for all other scripts.
This option can be used to implement more advanced caching of builds,
but can lead to unexpected states of the source directory.
When using this option, a build directory must be configured.
💥💣💥
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Environment=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-environment=\f[R]
Adds variables to the environment that package managers and the
prepare/build/postinstall/finalize scripts are executed with.
Takes a space\-separated list of variable assignments or just variable
names.
In the latter case, the values of those variables will be passed through
from the environment in which \f[B]mkosi\f[R] was invoked.
This option may be specified more than once, in which case all listed
variables will be set.
If the same variable is set twice, the later setting overrides the
earlier one.
.TP
\f[B]EnvironmentFiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-env\-file=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to files that contain environment
variable definitions to be added to the scripting environment.
Uses \f[B]mkosi.env\f[R] if it is found in the local directory.
The variables are first read from \f[B]mkosi.env\f[R] if it exists, then
from the given list of files and then from the \f[B]Environment=\f[R]
settings.
.TP
\f[B]WithTests=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-with\-tests=\f[R], \f[B]\-T\f[R]
If set to false (or when the command\-line option is used), the
\f[B]$WITH_TESTS\f[R] environment variable is set to \f[B]0\f[R] when
the \f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] scripts are invoked.
This is supposed to be used by the build scripts to bypass any unit or
integration tests that are normally run during the source build process.
Note that this option has no effect unless the \f[B]mkosi.build\f[R]
build scripts honor it.
.TP
\f[B]WithNetwork=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-with\-network=\f[R]
When true, enables network connectivity while the build scripts
\f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] are invoked.
By default, the build scripts run with networking turned off.
The \f[B]$WITH_NETWORK\f[R] environment variable is passed to the
\f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] build scripts indicating whether the build is done
with or without network.
.TP
\f[B]ProxyUrl=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-proxy\-url=\f[R]
Configure a proxy to be used for all outgoing network connections.
Various tools that \f[B]mkosi\f[R] invokes and for which the proxy can
be configured are configured to use this proxy.
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] also sets various well\-known environment variables to
specify the proxy to use for any programs it invokes that may need
internet access.
.TP
\f[B]ProxyExclude=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-proxy\-exclude=\f[R]
Configure hostnames for which requests should not go through the proxy.
Takes a comma\-separated list of hostnames.
.TP
\f[B]ProxyPeerCertificate=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-proxy\-peer\-certificate=\f[R]
Configure a file containing certificates used to verify the proxy.
Defaults to the system\-wide certificate store.
.RS
Currently, setting a proxy peer certificate is only supported when
\f[B]dnf\f[R] or \f[B]dnf5\f[R] is used to build the image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ProxyClientCertificate=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-proxy\-client\-certificate=\f[R]
Configure a file containing the certificate used to authenticate the
client with the proxy.
.RS
Currently, setting a proxy client certificate is only supported when
\f[B]dnf\f[R] or \f[B]dnf5\f[R] is used to build the image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ProxyClientKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-proxy\-client\-key=\f[R]
Configure a file containing the private key used to authenticate the
client with the proxy.
Defaults to the proxy client certificate if one is provided.
.RS
Currently, setting a proxy client key is only supported when
\f[B]dnf\f[R] or \f[B]dnf5\f[R] is used to build the image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]MakeScriptsExecutable=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-make\-scripts\-executable=\f[R]
If one of the hook scripts (see \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section) is not marked
as executable, attempt to chmod it instead of failing outright.
Defaults to \f[B]no\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]ForeignUIDRange=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-foreign\-uid\-range=\f[R]
If enabled, directory images will be owned by the foreign UID range on
disk instead of the user\(cqs UID.
.RS
Enabling this option is required to use directory images with \f[B]mkosi
shell\f[R], \f[B]mkosi boot\f[R] and \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R].
systemd\-nsresourced and systemd\-mountfsd v260 or newer are required on
the host to make use of this option.
.RE
.SS [Runtime] Section (previously known as the [Host] section)
.TP
\f[B]NSpawnSettings=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-settings=\f[R]
Specifies a \f[B].nspawn\f[R] settings file for
\f[B]systemd\-nspawn\f[R] to use in the \f[B]boot\f[R] and
\f[B]shell\f[R] verbs, and to place next to the generated image file.
This is useful to configure the \f[B]systemd\-nspawn\f[R] environment
when the image is run.
If this setting is not used but an \f[B]mkosi.nspawn\f[R] file found in
the local directory it is automatically used for this purpose.
.TP
\f[B]VirtualMachineMonitor=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-vmm=\f[R]
Configures the virtual machine monitor to use.
Takes one of \f[B]qemu\f[R] or \f[B]vmspawn\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]qemu\f[R].
.RS
When set to \f[B]qemu\f[R], the image is booted with \f[B]qemu\f[R].
Most output formats can be booted in \f[B]qemu\f[R].
Any arguments specified after the verb are appended to the
\f[B]qemu\f[R] invocation and are interpreted as extra \f[B]qemu\f[R]
command line arguments.
When set to \f[B]vmspawn\f[R], \f[B]systemd\-vmspawn\f[R] is used to
boot up the image, \f[B]vmspawn\f[R] only supports disk and directory
type images.
Any arguments specified after the verb are appended to the
\f[B]systemd\-vmspawn\f[R] invocation and are interpreted as extra
vmspawn options and extra kernel command line arguments.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Console=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-console=\f[R]
Configures how to set up the console of the VM.
Takes one of \f[B]interactive\f[R], \f[B]read\-only\f[R],
\f[B]native\f[R], \f[B]gui\f[R], or \f[B]headless\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]interactive\f[R].
\f[B]interactive\f[R] provides an interactive terminal interface to the
VM.
\f[B]read\-only\f[R] is similar, but is strictly read\-only, i.e.\ does
not accept any input from the user.
\f[B]native\f[R] also provides a TTY\-based interface, but uses
\f[B]qemu\f[R]\(cqs native implementation (which means the
\f[B]qemu\f[R] monitor is available).
\f[B]gui\f[R] shows the \f[B]qemu\f[R] graphical UI.
\f[B]headless\f[R] runs the VM without any console attached, useful for
fully automated or scripted VM usage.
\f[B]headless\f[R] is only supported by the \f[B]qemu\f[R] verb.
.TP
\f[B]CPUs=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-cpus=\f[R]
Configures the number of CPU cores to assign to the guest when booting a
virtual machine.
Defaults to \f[B]2\f[R].
.RS
When set to \f[B]0\f[R], the number of CPUs available to the
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] process will be used.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RAM=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-ram=\f[R]
Configures the amount of RAM assigned to the guest when booting a
virtual machine.
Defaults to \f[B]2G\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]MaxMem=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-maxmem=\f[R]
Configures the maximum amount of memory the guest may deploy in total
(RAM + hotplug memory devices).
Defaults to the amount of RAM configured.
.TP
\f[B]KVM=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-kvm=\f[R]
Configures whether KVM acceleration should be used when booting a
virtual machine.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]CXL=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-cxl=\f[R]
Configures whether CXL devices are enabled for a given machine.
Only valid if the architecture supports cxl.
Takes a boolean value.
Defaults to \f[B]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]VSock=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-vsock=\f[R]
Configures whether to provision a vsock when booting a virtual machine.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]VSockCID=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-vsock\-cid=\f[R]
Configures the vsock connection ID to use when booting a virtual
machine.
Takes a number in the interval \f[B][3, 0xFFFFFFFF)\f[R] or
\f[B]hash\f[R] or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
When set to \f[B]hash\f[R], the connection ID will be derived from the
full path to the image.
When set to \f[B]auto\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will try to find a free
connection ID automatically.
Otherwise, the provided number will be used as is.
.TP
\f[B]TPM=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tpm=\f[R]
Configure whether to use a virtual TPM when booting a virtual machine.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]Removable=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-removable=\f[R]
Configures whether to attach the image as a removable device when
booting a virtual machine.
Takes a boolean value.
Defaults to \f[B]no\f[R].
When enabled, the root disk is always attached as a SCSI device
regardless of the \f[B]DiskType=\f[R] setting.
.TP
\f[B]DiskType=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-disk\-type=\f[R]
Configures the disk type to use for the root disk when booting a virtual
machine.
Takes one of \f[B]virtio\-blk\f[R], \f[B]virtio\-scsi\f[R],
\f[B]nvme\f[R], or \f[B]scsi\-cd\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]virtio\-blk\f[R].
Use \f[B]scsi\-cd\f[R] to attach the image as a CD\-ROM drive, which is
useful for booting El Torito ISO images.
.TP
\f[B]Firmware=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-firmware=\f[R]
Configures the virtual machine firmware to use.
Takes one of \f[B]uefi\f[R], \f[B]uefi\-secure\-boot\f[R],
\f[B]linux\f[R], \f[B]linux\-noinitrd\f[R] or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
When set to \f[B]uefi\f[R], the OVMF firmware without secure boot
support is used.
When set to \f[B]uefi\-secure\-boot\f[R], the OVMF firmware with secure
boot support is used.
When set to \f[B]linux\f[R], direct kernel boot is used.
See the \f[B]Linux=\f[R] option for more details on which kernel image
is used with direct kernel boot.
\f[B]linux\-noinitrd\f[R] is identical to \f[B]linux\f[R] except that no
initrd is used.
When set to \f[B]auto\f[R], \f[B]uefi\-secure\-boot\f[R] is used if
possible and \f[B]linux\f[R] otherwise.
.TP
\f[B]FirmwareVariables=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-firmware\-variables=\f[R]
Configures the path to the virtual machine firmware variables file to
use.
Currently, this option is only taken into account when the
\f[B]uefi\f[R] or \f[B]uefi\-secure\-boot\f[R] firmware is used.
If not specified, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will search for the default variables
file and use that instead.
.RS
When set to \f[B]microsoft\f[R], a firmware variables file with the
Microsoft secure boot certificates already enrolled will be used.
When set to \f[B]microsoft\-mok\f[R], a firmware variables file with the
Microsoft secure boot certificates already enrolled will be extended
with a \f[B]MokList\f[R] variable containing the secure boot certificate
from \f[B]SecureBootCertificate=\f[R].
This is intended to be used together with shim binaries signed by the
distribution and locally signed EFI binaries.
When set to \f[B]custom\f[R], the secure boot certificate from
\f[B]SecureBootCertificate=\f[R] will be enrolled into the default
firmware variables file.
\f[B]virt\-fw\-vars\f[R] from the \c
.UR https://gitlab.com/kraxel/virt-firmware
virt\-firmware
.UE \c
\ project can be used to customize OVMF variable files.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Linux=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-linux=\f[R]
Set the kernel image to use for \f[B]qemu\f[R] direct kernel boot.
If not specified, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will use the kernel provided via the
command line (\f[B]\-kernel\f[R] option) or the latest kernel that was
installed into the image (or fail if no kernel was installed into the
image).
.RS
Note that when the \f[B]cpio\f[R] output format is used, direct kernel
boot is used regardless of the configured firmware.
Depending on the configured firmware, \f[B]qemu\f[R] might boot the
kernel itself or using the configured firmware.
This setting may include both the regular specifiers (see
\f[B]Specifiers\f[R]) and special delayed specifiers, that are expanded
after config parsing has finished, instead of during config parsing,
which are described below.
The following specifiers may be used:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Specifier
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\f[B]&&\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]&\f[R] character
T}
T{
\f[B]&b\f[R]
T}@T{
The final build directory (including subdirectory)
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Drives=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-drive=\f[R]
Add a drive.
Takes a colon\-delimited string of format
\f[B]<id>:<size>[:<directory>[:<options>[:<file\-id>[:<flags>]]]]\f[R].
\f[B]id\f[R] specifies the ID assigned to the drive.
This can be used as the \f[B]drive=\f[R] property in various
\f[B]qemu\f[R] devices.
\f[B]size\f[R] specifies the size of the drive.
This takes a size in bytes.
Additionally, the suffixes \f[B]K\f[R], \f[B]M\f[R] and \f[B]G\f[R] can
be used to specify a size in kilobytes, megabytes and gigabytes
respectively.
\f[B]directory\f[R] optionally specifies the directory in which to
create the file backing the drive.
If unset, the file will be created under \f[B]/var/tmp\f[R].
\f[B]options\f[R] optionally specifies extra comma\-delimited properties
which are passed verbatim to \f[B]qemu\f[R]\(cqs \f[B]\-blockdev\f[R]
option.
\f[B]file\-id\f[R] specifies the ID of the file backing the drive.
If unset, this defaults to the drive ID.
Drives with the same file ID will share the backing file.
The directory and size of the file will be determined from the first
drive with a given file ID.
\f[B]flags\f[R] takes a comma\-separated list of drive flags which
currently only supports \f[B]persist\f[R].
\f[B]persist\f[R] determines whether the drive will be persisted across
\f[B]qemu\f[R] invocations.
The files backing the drives will be created with the schema
\f[B]/<directory>/mkosi\-drive\-<machine\-or\-image\-name>\-<file\-id>\f[R].
You can skip values by setting them to the empty string, specifying
e.g.\ \f[B]myfs:1G::::persist\f[R] will create a persistent drive under
\f[B]/var/tmp/mkosi\-drive\-main\-myfs\f[R].
.RS
\f[B]Example usage:\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Runtime]\f[R]
Drives=btrfs:10G
       ext4:20G
QemuArgs=\-device nvme,serial=btrfs,drive=btrfs
         \-device nvme,serial=ext4,drive=ext4
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]QemuArgs=\f[R]
Space\-delimited list of additional arguments to pass when invoking
\f[B]qemu\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]Ephemeral=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-ephemeral=\f[R]
When used with the \f[B]shell\f[R], \f[B]boot\f[R], or \f[B]vm\f[R]
verbs, this option runs the specified verb on a temporary snapshot of
the output image that is removed immediately when the container
terminates.
Taking the temporary snapshot is more efficient on file systems that
support reflinks natively (\f[B]btrfs\f[R] or \f[B]xfs\f[R]) than on
more traditional file systems that do not (ext4).
.TP
\f[B]Credentials=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-credential=\f[R]
Set credentials to be passed to \f[B]systemd\-nspawn\f[R] or the virtual
machine respectively when \f[B]mkosi shell/boot\f[R] or \f[B]mkosi
vm\f[R] are used.
This option takes a space separated list of values which can be either
key=value pairs or paths.
If a path is provided, if it is a file, the credential name will be the
name of the file.
If the file is executable, the credential value will be the output of
executing the file.
Otherwise, the credential value will be the contents of the file.
If the path is a directory, the same logic applies to each file in the
directory.
.RS
Note that values will only be treated as paths if they do not contain
the delimiter (\f[B]=\f[R]).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]KernelCommandLineExtra=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-kernel\-command\-line\-extra=\f[R]
Set extra kernel command line entries that are appended to the kernel
command line at runtime when booting the image.
When booting in a container, these are passed as extra arguments to
systemd.
When booting in a VM, these are appended to the kernel command line via
the SMBIOS io.systemd.stub.kernel\-cmdline\-extra OEM string.
This will only be picked up by \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R] and
\f[B]systemd\-stub\f[R] versions newer than or equal to v254.
.TP
\f[B]RuntimeTrees=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-runtime\-tree=\f[R]
Takes a colon\-separated pair of paths.
The first path refers to a directory to mount into any machine
(container or VM) started by mkosi.
The second path refers to the target directory inside the machine.
If the second path is not provided, the directory is mounted at
\f[B]/root/src\f[R] in the machine.
If the second path is relative, it is interpreted relative to
\f[B]/root/src\f[R] in the machine.
.RS
For each mounted directory, the uid and gid of the user running mkosi
are mapped to the root user in the machine.
This means that all the files and directories will appear as if
they\(cqre owned by root in the machine, and all new files and
directories created by root in the machine in these directories will be
owned by the user running mkosi on the host.
Note that when using \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R] with this feature systemd v254
or newer has to be installed in the image.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RuntimeSize=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-runtime\-size=\f[R]
If specified, disk images are grown to the specified size when
they\(cqre booted with \f[B]mkosi boot\f[R] or \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R].
Takes a size in bytes.
Additionally, the suffixes \f[B]K\f[R], \f[B]M\f[R] and \f[B]G\f[R] can
be used to specify a size in kilobytes, megabytes and gigabytes
respectively.
.TP
\f[B]RuntimeNetwork=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-runtime\-network=\f[R]
Takes one of \f[B]user\f[R], \f[B]interface\f[R] or \f[B]none\f[R].
Defaults to \f[B]user\f[R].
Specifies the networking to set up when booting the image.
\f[B]user\f[R] sets up usermode networking.
\f[B]interface\f[R] sets up a virtual network connection between the
host and the image.
This translates to a veth interface for \f[B]mkosi shell\f[R] and
\f[B]mkosi boot\f[R] and a tap interface for \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R] and
\f[B]mkosi vmspawn\f[R].
.RS
Note that when using \f[B]interface\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\f[R] does not
automatically configure the host interface.
It is expected that a recent version of \f[B]systemd\-networkd\f[R] is
running on the host which will automatically configure the host
interface of the link.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RuntimeBuildSources=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-runtime\-build\-sources=\f[R]
Mount the build sources configured with \f[B]BuildSources=\f[R] and the
build directory (if one is configured) to the same locations in
\f[B]/work\f[R] that they were mounted to when running the build script
when using \f[B]mkosi boot\f[R] or \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]BindUser=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-bind\-user=\f[R]
Bind the home directory of the current user into the container/vm.
Takes a boolean.
Disabled by default.
.TP
\f[B]SshKey=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-ssh\-key=\f[R]
Path to the X.509 private key in PEM format to use to connect to a
virtual machine started with \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R] and built with the
\f[B]Ssh=\f[R] option enabled (or with \f[B]systemd\-ssh\-generator\f[R]
installed) via the \f[B]mkosi ssh\f[R] command.
If not configured and \f[B]mkosi.key\f[R] exists in the working
directory, it will automatically be used for this purpose.
Run \f[B]mkosi genkey\f[R] to automatically generate a key in
\f[B]mkosi.key\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SshCertificate=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-ssh\-certificate=\f[R]
Path to the X.509 certificate in PEM format to provision as the SSH
public key in virtual machines started with \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R].
If not configured and \f[B]mkosi.crt\f[R] exists in the working
directory, it will automatically be used for this purpose.
Run \f[B]mkosi genkey\f[R] to automatically generate a certificate in
\f[B]mkosi.crt\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]Machine=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-machine=\f[R]
Specify the machine name to use when booting the image.
Can also be used to refer to a specific image when SSH\-ing into an
image (e.g.
\f[B]mkosi \-\-image=myimage ssh\f[R]).
.RS
Note that \f[B]Ephemeral=\f[R] has to be enabled to start multiple
instances of the same image.
Defaults to the current working directory name.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Register=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-register=\f[R]
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
Specifies whether to register the vm/container with systemd\-machined.
If enabled, mkosi will fail if it can\(cqt register the vm/container
with systemd\-machined.
If disabled, mkosi will not register the vm/container with
systemd\-machined.
If \f[B]auto\f[R], mkosi will register the vm/container with
systemd\-machined if it is available.
Defaults to \f[B]auto\f[R].
.RS
Note that mkosi will try to register the machine with systemd\-machined
in the user session.
systemd v259 or newer is required to have systemd\-machined be available
in user sessions.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ForwardJournal=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-forward\-journal=\f[R]
Specify the path to which journal logs from containers and virtual
machines should be forwarded.
If the path has the \f[B].journal\f[R] extension, it is interpreted as a
file to which the journal should be written.
Otherwise, the path is interpreted as a directory to which the journal
should be written.
.RS
Note that systemd v256 or newer is required in the virtual machine for
log forwarding to work.
Note that if a path with the \f[B].journal\f[R] extension is given, the
journal size is limited to \f[B]4G\f[R].
Configure an output directory instead of file if your workload produces
more than \f[B]4G\f[R] worth of journal data.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]StorageTargetMode=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-storage\-target\-mode=\f[R]
Specifies whether the \f[B]serve\f[R] verb should start
\f[B]systemd\-storagetm\f[R] to serve disk images over NVME\-TCP.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
If enabled, systemd\-storagetm is always started and mkosi will fail if
it cannot start systemd\-storagetm.
If disabled, systemd\-storagetm is never started.
If \f[B]auto\f[R], systemd\-storagetm will be started if a disk image is
being built, the systemd\-storagetm binary is found and \f[B]mkosi
serve\f[R] is being invoked as the root user.
.TP
\f[B]SysupdateDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-sysupdate\-directory=\f[R]
Path to a directory containing systemd\-sysupdate transfer definition
files that are used by \f[B]mkosi sysupdate\f[R].
If \f[B]mkosi.sysupdate/\f[R] exists in the local directory, it will be
used for this purpose as well.
.RS
Note that \f[B]mkosi sysupdate\f[R] invokes \f[B]systemd\-sysupdate\f[R]
with \f[B]\-\-transfer\-source=\f[R] set to the \f[B]mkosi\f[R] output
directory.
To make use of this in a transfer definition file, set
\f[B]PathRelativeTo=explicit\f[R] to have the \f[B]Path=\f[R] setting
for the transfer source be interpreted relative to the \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
output directory.
Generally, configuring \f[B]PathRelativeTo=explicit\f[R] and
\f[B]Path=/\f[R] for the transfer source is sufficient for the match
pattern to be interpreted relative to the \f[B]mkosi\f[R] output
directory.
.RE
.SS [Match] Section
.TP
\f[B]Profiles=\f[R]
Matches against the configured profiles.
.TP
\f[B]Distribution=\f[R]
Matches against the configured distribution.
.TP
\f[B]Release=\f[R]
Matches against the configured distribution release.
If this condition is used and no distribution has been explicitly
configured yet, the host distribution and release are used.
.TP
\f[B]Architecture=\f[R]
Matches against the configured architecture.
If this condition is used and no architecture has been explicitly
configured yet, the host architecture is used.
.RS
\f[B]Architecture=uefi\f[R] can be used to match against any
architecture that supports UEFI.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Repositories=\f[R]
Matches against repositories enabled with the \f[B]Repositories=\f[R]
setting.
Takes a single repository name.
.TP
\f[B]OutputFormat=\f[R]
Matches against the configured value for \f[B]OutputFormat=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]PathExists=\f[R]
This condition is satisfied if the given path exists.
Relative paths are interpreted relative to the parent directory of the
config file that the condition is read from.
.TP
\f[B]ImageId=\f[R]
Matches against the configured image ID, supporting globs.
If this condition is used and no image ID has been explicitly configured
yet, this condition fails.
.TP
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R]
Matches against the configured image version.
Image versions can be prepended by the operators \f[B]==\f[R],
\f[B]!=\f[R], \f[B]>=\f[R], \f[B]<=\f[R], \f[B]<\f[R], \f[B]>\f[R] for
rich version comparisons according to the UAPI group version format
specification.
If no operator is prepended, the equality operator is assumed by
default.
If this condition is used and no image version has been explicitly
configured yet, this condition fails.
.TP
\f[B]Bootable=\f[R]
Matches against the configured value for the \f[B]Bootable=\f[R]
feature.
Takes a boolean value or \f[B]auto\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]Bootloader=\f[R]
Matches against the configured value for \f[B]Bootloader=\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]Format=\f[R]
Matches against the configured value for the \f[B]Format=\f[R] option.
Takes an output format (see the \f[B]Format=\f[R] option).
.TP
\f[B]SystemdVersion=\f[R]
Matches against the systemd version on the host (as reported by
\f[B]systemctl \-\-version\f[R]).
Values can be prepended by the operators \f[B]==\f[R], \f[B]!=\f[R],
\f[B]>=\f[R], \f[B]<=\f[R], \f[B]<\f[R], \f[B]>\f[R] for rich version
comparisons according to the UAPI group version format specification.
If no operator is prepended, the equality operator is assumed by
default.
.TP
\f[B]BuildSources=\f[R]
Takes a build source target path (see \f[B]BuildSources=\f[R]).
This match is satisfied if any of the configured build sources uses this
target path.
For example, if we have a \f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R] file containing:
.RS
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Build]\f[R]
BuildSources=../abc/qed:kernel
.EE
and a drop\-in containing:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Match]\f[R]
BuildSources=kernel
.EE
The drop\-in will be included.
Any absolute paths passed to this setting are interpreted relative to
the current working directory.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]HostArchitecture=\f[R]
Matches against the host\(cqs native architecture.
See the \f[B]Architecture=\f[R] setting for a list of possible values.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeDistribution=\f[R]
Matches against the configured tools tree distribution.
.TP
\f[B]ToolsTreeRelease=\f[R]
Matches against the configured tools tree release.
.TP
\f[B]Environment=\f[R]
Matches against a specific key/value pair configured with
\f[B]Environment=\f[R].
If no value is provided, check if the given key is in the environment
regardless of which value it has.
.TP
\f[B]Image=\f[R]
Match against the current (sub)image name.
The name of a subimage is its name in \f[B]mkosi.images/\f[R] (without
any \f[B].conf\f[R] suffix).
The name of the top level image is \f[B]main\f[R].
The main use case is to allow having a shared config that can be
included by both the top level image and subimages by gating the
universal settings behind a \f[B]Image=main\f[R] match.
.PP
This table shows which matchers support globs, rich comparisons and the
default value that is matched against if no value has been configured at
the time the config file is read:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(13.1n) lw(3.5n) lw(9.1n) lw(44.3n).
