.TE
.SS [Include]
.TP
\f[B]Include=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-include=\f[R], \f[B]\-I\f[R]
Include extra configuration from the given file or directory.
The extra configuration is included immediately after parsing the
setting, except when used on the command line, in which case the extra
configuration is included after parsing all command line arguments.
.RS
Note that each path containing extra configuration is only parsed once,
even if included more than once with \f[B]Include=\f[R].
The builtin configs for the \f[B]mkosi\f[R] default initrd, default
tools tree, default virtual machine image and default UKI addon can be
included by including the literal value \f[B]mkosi\-initrd\f[R],
\f[B]mkosi\-tools\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\-vm\f[R] or \f[B]mkosi\-addon\f[R]
respectively.
Note: Include names starting with either of the literals
\f[B]mkosi\-\f[R] or \f[B]contrib\-\f[R] are reserved for use by
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] itself.
.RE
.SS [Config] Section
.TP
\f[B]Profiles=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-profile=\f[R]
Select the given profiles.
A profile is a configuration file or directory in the
\f[B]mkosi.profiles/\f[R] directory.
The configuration files and directories of each profile are included
after parsing the \f[B]mkosi.conf.d/*.conf\f[R] drop in configuration.
.TP
\f[B]Dependencies=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dependency=\f[R]
The images that this image depends on specified as a comma\-separated
list.
All images configured in this option will be built before this image.
.RS
When this setting is specified for the \(lqmain\(rq image, it specifies
which subimages should be built.
See the \f[B]BUILDING MULTIPLE IMAGES\f[R] section for more information.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]MinimumVersion=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-minimum\-version=\f[R]
The minimum \f[B]mkosi\f[R] version required to build this
configuration.
If specified multiple times, the highest specified version is used.
.RS
The minimum version can also be specified as a git commit hash when
prefixed with \f[B]commit:\f[R], in which case mkosi must be executed
from a git checkout and the specified git commit hash must be an
ancestor of the currently checked out git commit in the repository that
mkosi is being executed from.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ConfigureScripts=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-configure\-script=\f[R]
Takes a comma\-separated list of paths to executables that are used as
the configure scripts for this image.
Glob patterns are supported and matching paths are sorted
alphabetically.
See the \f[B]SCRIPTS\f[R] section for more information.
.TP
\f[B]PassEnvironment=\f[R], \f[B]\-\-pass\-environment=\f[R]
Takes a list of environment variable names separated by spaces.
When building multiple images, pass the listed environment variables to
each individual subimage as if they were \(lquniversal\(rq settings.
See the \f[B]BUILDING MULTIPLE IMAGES\f[R] section for more information.
.SS [UKIProfile] Section
The \f[B]UKIProfile\f[R] section can be used in UKI profile config files
which are passed to the \f[B]UnifiedKernelImageProfiles=\f[R] setting.
The following settings can be specified in the \f[B]UKIProfile\f[R]
section:
.TP
\f[B]Profile=\f[R]
The contents of the \f[B].profile\f[R] section of the UKI profile.
Takes a list of key/value pairs separated by \f[B]=\f[R].
The \f[B]ID=\f[R] key must be specified.
See the UKI \c
.UR https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/unified_kernel_image/#multi-profile-ukis
specification
.UE \c
\ for a full list of possible keys.
.TP
\f[B]Cmdline=\f[R]
Extra kernel command line options for the UKI profile.
Takes a space delimited list of extra kernel command line arguments.
Note that the final \f[B].cmdline\f[R] section will the combination of
the base \f[B].cmdline\f[R] section and the extra kernel command line
arguments specified with this setting.
.TP
\f[B]SignExpectedPcr=\f[R]
Sign expected PCR measurements for this UKI profile.
Takes a boolean.
Enabled by default.
.SS Specifiers
The current value of various settings can be accessed when parsing
configuration files by using specifiers.
To write a literal \f[B]%\f[R] character in a configuration file without
treating it as a specifier, use \f[B]%%\f[R].
The following specifiers are understood:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Setting
T}@T{
Specifier
T}
_
T{
\f[B]Distribution=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%d\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]Release=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%r\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]Architecture=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%a\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]Format=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%t\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]Output=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%o\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]OutputDirectory=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%O\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]ImageId=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%i\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]%v\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
There are also specifiers that are independent of settings:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Specifier
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\f[B]%C\f[R]
T}@T{
Parent directory of current config file
T}
T{
\f[B]%P\f[R]
T}@T{
Current working directory
T}
T{
\f[B]%D\f[R]
T}@T{
Directory that \f[B]mkosi\f[R] was invoked in
T}
T{
\f[B]%I\f[R]
T}@T{
Name of the current subimage in \f[B]mkosi.images\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
Finally, there are specifiers that are derived from a setting:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Specifier
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\f[B]%F\f[R]
T}@T{
The default filesystem of the configured distribution
T}
.TE
.PP
Note that the current working directory changes as \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
parses its configuration.
Specifically, each time \f[B]mkosi\f[R] parses a directory containing a
\f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R] file, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] changes its working directory
to that directory.
.PP
Note that the directory that \f[B]mkosi\f[R] was invoked in is
influenced by the \f[B]\-\-directory=\f[R] command line argument.
.PP
The following table shows example values for the directory specifiers
listed above:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(4.7n) lw(13.4n) lw(25.2n) lw(26.7n).
T{
T}@T{
\f[B]$D/mkosi.conf\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D/mkosi.conf.d/abc/abc.conf\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D/mkosi.conf.d/abc/mkosi.conf\f[R]
T}
_
T{
\f[B]%C\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D/mkosi.conf.d\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D/mkosi.conf.d/abc\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]%P\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D/mkosi.conf.d/abc\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[B]%D\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]$D\f[R]
T}
.TE
.SS Supported distributions
Images may be created containing installations of the following
distributions:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Fedora Linux\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Debian\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Kali Linux\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Ubuntu\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Arch Linux\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]openSUSE\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Mageia\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]CentOS\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]RHEL\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]RHEL UBI\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]OpenMandriva\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Rocky Linux\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Alma Linux\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]Azure Linux\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]postmarketOS\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[I]None\f[R] (\f[B]Requires the user to provide a pre\-built
rootfs\f[R])
.PP
In theory, any distribution may be used on the host for building images
containing any other distribution, as long as the necessary tools are
available.
Specifically, any distribution that packages \f[B]apt\f[R] may be used
to build \f[I]Debian\f[R], \f[I]Kali\f[R] or \f[I]Ubuntu\f[R] images.
Any distribution that packages \f[B]dnf\f[R] may be used to build images
for any of the RPM\-based distributions.
Any distro that packages \f[B]pacman\f[R] may be used to build \f[I]Arch
Linux\f[R] images.
Any distribution that packages \f[B]zypper\f[R] may be used to build
\f[I]openSUSE\f[R] images.
Other distributions and build automation tools for embedded Linux
systems such as Buildroot, OpenEmbedded and Yocto Project may be used by
selecting the \f[B]custom\f[R] distribution, and populating the rootfs
via a combination of base trees, skeleton trees, and prepare scripts.
.PP
Currently, \f[I]Fedora Linux\f[R] packages all relevant tools as of
Fedora 28.
.PP
Note that when not using a custom mirror, \f[B]RHEL\f[R] images can only
be built from a host system with a \f[B]RHEL\f[R] subscription
(established using e.g.\ \f[B]subscription\-manager\f[R]).
.SH EXECUTION FLOW
Execution flow for \f[B]mkosi build\f[R].
Default values/calls are shown in parentheses.
When building with \f[B]\-\-incremental=yes\f[R] \f[B]mkosi\f[R] creates
a cache of the distribution installation if not already existing and
replaces the distribution installation in consecutive runs with data
from the cached one.
.IP "1." 3
Parse CLI options
.IP "2." 3
Parse configuration files
.IP "3." 3
Run configure scripts (\f[B]mkosi.configure\f[R])
.IP "4." 3
If we\(cqre not running as root, unshare the user namespace and map the
subuid range configured in \f[B]/etc/subuid\f[R] and
\f[B]/etc/subgid\f[R] into it.
.IP "5." 3
Unshare the mount namespace
.IP "6." 3
Remount the following directories read\-only if they exist:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/usr\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/etc\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/opt\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/srv\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/boot\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/efi\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/media\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]/mnt\f[R]
.RE
.PP
Then, for each image, we execute the following steps:
.IP " 1." 4
Copy sandbox trees into the workspace
.IP " 2." 4
Sync the package manager repository metadata
.IP " 3." 4
Run sync scripts (\f[B]mkosi.sync\f[R])
.IP " 4." 4
Copy base trees (\f[B]\-\-base\-tree=\f[R]) into the image
.IP " 5." 4
Reuse a cached image if one is available
.IP " 6." 4
Copy a snapshot of the package manager repository metadata into the
image
.IP " 7." 4
Copy skeleton trees (\f[B]mkosi.skeleton\f[R]) into image
.IP " 8." 4
Install distribution and packages into image
.IP " 9." 4
Run prepare scripts on image with the \f[B]final\f[R] argument
(\f[B]mkosi.prepare\f[R])
.IP "10." 4
Install build packages in overlay if any build scripts are configured
.IP "11." 4
Run prepare scripts on overlay with the \f[B]build\f[R] argument if any
build scripts are configured (\f[B]mkosi.prepare\f[R])
.IP "12." 4
Cache the image if configured (\f[B]\-\-incremental=yes\f[R])
.IP "13." 4
Run build scripts on image + overlay if any build scripts are configured
(\f[B]mkosi.build\f[R])
.IP "14." 4
Finalize the build if the output format \f[B]none\f[R] is configured
.IP "15." 4
Copy the build scripts outputs into the image
.IP "16." 4
Copy the extra trees into the image (\f[B]mkosi.extra\f[R])
.IP "17." 4
Run post\-install scripts (\f[B]mkosi.postinst\f[R])
.IP "18." 4
Write config files required for \f[B]Ssh=\f[R], \f[B]Autologin=\f[R] and
\f[B]MakeInitrd=\f[R]
.IP "19." 4
Install systemd\-boot and configure secure boot if configured
(\f[B]\-\-secure\-boot=yes\f[R])
.IP "20." 4
Run \f[B]systemd\-sysusers\f[R]
.IP "21." 4
Run \f[B]systemd\-tmpfiles\f[R]
.IP "22." 4
Run \f[B]systemctl preset\-all\f[R]
.IP "23." 4
Run \f[B]depmod\f[R]
.IP "24." 4
Run \f[B]systemd\-firstboot\f[R]
.IP "25." 4
Run \f[B]systemd\-hwdb\f[R]
.IP "26." 4
Remove packages and files (\f[B]RemovePackages=\f[R],
\f[B]RemoveFiles=\f[R])
.IP "27." 4
Run SELinux relabel if a SELinux policy is installed
.IP "28." 4
Run finalize scripts (\f[B]mkosi.finalize\f[R])
.IP "29." 4
Generate unified kernel image if configured to do so
.IP "30." 4
Generate final output format
.IP "31." 4
Run post\-output scripts (\f[B]mkosi.postoutput\f[R])
.SH SCRIPTS
To allow for image customization that cannot be implemented using
\f[B]mkosi\f[R]\(cqs builtin features, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] supports running
scripts at various points during the image build process that can
customize the image as needed.
Scripts are executed on the host system as root (either real root or
root within the user namespace that \f[B]mkosi\f[R] created when running
unprivileged) with a customized environment to simplify modifying the
image.
For each script, the configured build sources (\f[B]BuildSources=\f[R])
are mounted into the current working directory before running the script
in the current working directory.
\f[B]$SRCDIR\f[R] is set to point to the current working directory.
The following scripts are supported:
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.configure\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]ConfigureScripts=\f[R])
exists, it is executed before building the image.
This script may be used to dynamically modify the configuration.
It receives the configuration serialized as JSON on stdin and should
output the modified configuration serialized as JSON on stdout.
Note that this script only runs when building or booting the image
(\f[B]build\f[R], \f[B]vm\f[R], \f[B]boot\f[R] and \f[B]shell\f[R]
verbs).
If a default tools tree is configured, it will be built before running
the configure scripts and the configure scripts will run with the tools
tree available.
This also means that the modifications made by configure scripts will
not be visible in the \f[B]summary\f[R] output.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.sync\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]SyncScripts=\f[R]) exists, it is
executed before the image is built.
This script may be used to update various sources that are used to build
the image.
One use case is to run \f[B]git pull\f[R] on various source repositories
before building the image.
Specifically, the \f[B]BuildSourcesEphemeral=\f[R] setting does not
apply to sync scripts, which means sync scripts can be used to update
build sources even if \f[B]BuildSourcesEphemeral=\f[R] is enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.prepare\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]PrepareScripts=\f[R]) exists,
it is first called with the \f[B]final\f[R] argument, right after the
software packages are installed.
It is called a second time with the \f[B]build\f[R] command line
parameter, right after the build packages are installed and the build
overlay mounted on top of the image\(cqs root directory .
This script has network access and may be used to install packages from
other sources than the distro\(cqs package manager (e.g.\ \f[B]pip\f[R],
\f[B]npm\f[R], \&...), after all software packages are installed but
before the image is cached (if incremental mode is enabled).
In contrast to a general purpose installation, it is safe to install
packages to the system (\f[B]pip install\f[R], \f[B]npm install
\-g\f[R]) instead of in \f[B]$SRCDIR\f[R] itself because the build image
is only used for a single project and can easily be thrown away and
rebuilt so there\(cqs no risk of conflicting dependencies and no risk of
polluting the host system.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.build\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]BuildScripts=\f[R]) exists, it
is executed with the build overlay mounted on top of the image\(cqs root
directory.
When running the build script, \f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R] points to a directory
where the script should place any files generated it would like to end
up in the image.
Note that \f[B]make\f[R]\-, \f[B]automake\f[R]\-, and
\f[B]meson\f[R]\-based build systems generally honor \f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R],
thus making it very natural to build \f[I]source\f[R] trees from the
build script.
After running the build script, the contents of \f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R] are
copied into the image.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.postinst\f[B]\f[R]
(\f[B]PostInstallationScripts=\f[R]) exists, it is executed after the
(optional) build tree and extra trees have been installed.
This script may be used to alter the images without any restrictions,
after all software packages and built sources have been installed.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.finalize\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]FinalizeScripts=\f[R])
exists, it is executed as the last step of preparing an image.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.postoutput\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]PostOutputScripts=\f[R])
exists, it is executed right after all the output files have been
generated, before they are finally moved into the output directory.
This can be used to generate additional or alternative outputs,
e.g.\ \f[B]SHA256FILES\f[R] or SBOM manifests.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.clean\f[B]\f[R] (\f[B]CleanScripts=\f[R]) exists, it
is executed right after the outputs of a previous build have been
cleaned up.
A clean script can clean up any outputs that \f[B]mkosi\f[R] does not
know about (e.g.
artifacts from \f[B]SplitArtifacts=partitions\f[R] or RPMs built in a
build script).
Note that this script does not use the tools tree even if one is
configured.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.version\f[B]\f[R] exists and is executable, it is run
during configuration parsing and populates \f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R] with
the output on stdout.
This can be used for external version tracking, e.g.\ with \f[B]git
describe\f[R] or \f[B]date \(aq+%Y\-%m\-%d\(aq\f[R].
Note that this script is executed on the host system without any
sandboxing.
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.rootpw\f[B]\f[R] exists and is executable, it is run
during configuration parsing and populates \f[B]RootPassword=\f[R] with
the output on stdout.
This can be used to randomly generate a password and can be remembered
by outputting it to stderr or by reading \f[B]$MKOSI_CONFIG\f[R] in
another script (e.g.\ \f[B]mkosi.postoutput\f[R]).
Note that this script is executed on the host system without any
sandboxing.
.PP
If a script uses the \f[B].chroot\f[R] extension, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will
chroot into the image using \f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R] (see below) before
executing the script.
For example, if \f[B]mkosi.postinst.chroot\f[R] exists, \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
will chroot into the image and execute it as the post\-installation
script.
.PP
Instead of a single file script, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will also read all
files in lexicographical order from appropriately named \f[B].d\f[R]
directories, e.g.\ all files in a \f[B]mkosi.build.d\f[R] would be used
as build scripts.
This is supported by
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.sync.d\f[R],
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.prepare.d\f[R],
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.build.d\f[R],
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.postinst.d\f[R],
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.finalize.d\f[R],
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.postoutput.d\f[R], and
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi.clean.d\f[R].
.PP
This can be combined with the \f[B].chroot\f[R] extension,
e.g.\ \f[B]mkosi.build.d/01\-foo.sh\f[R] would be run without chrooting
into the image and \f[B]mkosi.build.d/02\-bar.sh.chroot\f[R] would be
run after chrooting into the image first.
.PP
Scripts executed by \f[B]mkosi\f[R] receive the following environment
variables:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$ARCHITECTURE\f[R] contains the architecture from the
\f[B]Architecture=\f[R] setting.
If \f[B]Architecture=\f[R] is not set, it will contain the native
architecture of the host machine.
See the documentation of \f[B]Architecture=\f[R] for possible values for
this variable.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$QEMU_ARCHITECTURE\f[R] contains the architecture from
\f[B]$ARCHITECTURE\f[R] in the format used by \f[B]qemu\f[R].
Useful for finding the qemu binary (
\f[B]qemu\-system\-$QEMU_ARCHITECTURE\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$EFI_ARCHITECTURE\f[R] contains the architecture from
\f[B]$ARCHITECTURE\f[R] in the format used by \f[B]UEFI\f[R].
It is unset on architectures that do not support \f[B]UEFI\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$DISTRIBUTION\f[R] contains the distribution from the
\f[B]Distribution=\f[R] setting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$RELEASE\f[R] contains the release from the \f[B]Release=\f[R]
setting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$DISTRIBUTION_ARCHITECTURE\f[R] contains the architecture from
\f[B]$ARCHITECTURE\f[R] in the format used by the configured
distribution.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$PROFILES\f[R] contains the profiles from the \f[B]Profiles=\f[R]
setting as a comma\-delimited string.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$CACHED\f[R] is set to \f[B]1\f[R] if a cached image is available,
\f[B]0\f[R] otherwise.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$CHROOT_SCRIPT\f[R] contains the path to the running script
relative to the image root directory.
The primary usecase for this variable is in combination with the
\f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R] script.
See the description of \f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R] below for more
information.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$SRCDIR\f[R] contains the path to the directory \f[B]mkosi\f[R] was
invoked from, with any configured build sources mounted on top.
\f[B]$CHROOT_SRCDIR\f[R] contains the value that \f[B]$SRCDIR\f[R] will
have after invoking \f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$BUILDDIR\f[R] is only defined if \f[B]mkosi.builddir\f[R] exists
and points to the build directory to use.
This is useful for all build systems that support out\-of\-tree builds
to reuse already built artifacts from previous runs.
\f[B]$CHROOT_BUILDDIR\f[R] contains the value that \f[B]$BUILDDIR\f[R]
will have after invoking \f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R] is a directory into which any installed software
generated by a build script may be placed.
This variable is only set when executing a build script.
\f[B]$CHROOT_DESTDIR\f[R] contains the value that \f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R]
will have after invoking \f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$OUTPUTDIR\f[R] points to the staging directory used to store build
artifacts generated during the build.
\f[B]$CHROOT_OUTPUTDIR\f[R] contains the value that \f[B]$OUTPUTDIR\f[R]
will have after invoking \f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$PACKAGEDIR\f[R] points to the directory containing the local
package repository.
Build scripts can add more packages to the local repository by writing
the packages to \f[B]$PACKAGEDIR\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$ARTIFACTDIR\f[R] points to the directory that is used to pass
around build artifacts generated during the build and make them
available for use by mkosi.
This is similar to \f[B]PACKAGEDIR\f[R], but is meant for artifacts that
may not be packages understood by the package manager, e.g.\ initrds
created by other initrd generators than mkosi.
Build scripts can add more artifacts to the directory by placing them in
\f[B]$ARTIFACTDIR\f[R].
Files in this directory are only available for the current build and are
not copied out like the contents of \f[B]$OUTPUTDIR\f[R].
.RS 2
.PP
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will also use certain subdirectories of an artifacts
directory to automatically use their contents at certain steps.
Currently the following two subdirectories in the artifact directory are
used by mkosi:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]io.mkosi.microcode\f[R]: All files in this directory are used as
microcode files, i.e.\ they are prepended to the initrds in
lexicographical order.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]io.mkosi.initrd\f[R]: All files in this directory are used as
initrds and joined in lexicographical order.
.PP
It is recommended, that users of \f[B]$ARTIFACTDIR\f[R] put things for
their own use in a similar namespaced directory,
e.g.\ \f[B]local.my.namespace\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$BUILDROOT\f[R] is the root directory of the image being built,
optionally with the build overlay mounted on top depending on the script
that\(cqs being executed.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$WITH_DOCS\f[R] is either \f[B]0\f[R] or \f[B]1\f[R] depending on
whether a build without or with installed documentation was requested
(\f[B]WithDocs=yes\f[R]).
A build script should suppress installation of any package documentation
to \f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R] in case \f[B]$WITH_DOCS\f[R] is set to
\f[B]0\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$WITH_TESTS\f[R] is either \f[B]0\f[R] or \f[B]1\f[R] depending on
whether a build without or with running the test suite was requested
(\f[B]WithTests=no\f[R]).
A build script should avoid running any unit or integration tests in
case \f[B]$WITH_TESTS\f[R] is \f[B]0\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$WITH_NETWORK\f[R] is either \f[B]0\f[R] or \f[B]1\f[R] depending
on whether a build without or with networking is being executed
(\f[B]WithNetwork=no\f[R]).
A build script should avoid any network communication in case
\f[B]$WITH_NETWORK\f[R] is \f[B]0\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\f[R] is defined if requested
(\f[B]SourceDateEpoch=TIMESTAMP\f[R],
\f[B]Environment=SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=TIMESTAMP\f[R] or the host
environment variable \f[B]$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\f[R]).
This is useful to make builds reproducible.
See \c
.UR https://reproducible-builds.org/specs/source-date-epoch/
SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
.UE \c
\ for more information.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_UID\f[R] and \f[B]$MKOSI_GID\f[R] respectively are the uid,
gid of the user that invoked mkosi.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_CONFIG\f[R] is a file containing a json summary of the
settings of the current image.
This file can be parsed inside scripts to gain access to all settings
for the current image.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$IMAGE_ID\f[R] contains the identifier from the \f[B]ImageId=\f[R]
or \f[B]\-\-image\-id=\f[R] setting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$IMAGE_VERSION\f[R] contains the version from the
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-image\-version=\f[R] setting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_DEBUG\f[R] is either \f[B]0\f[R] or \f[B]1\f[R] depending on
whether debugging output is enabled.
