.TE
.PP
Additionally, when a script is executed, a few scripts are made
available via \f[B]$PATH\f[R] to simplify common usecases.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R]: This script will chroot into the image and
execute the given command.
On top of chrooting into the image, it will also mount various files and
directories (\f[B]$SRCDIR\f[R], \f[B]$DESTDIR\f[R], \f[B]$BUILDDIR\f[R],
\f[B]$OUTPUTDIR\f[R], \f[B]$CHROOT_SCRIPT\f[R]) into the image and
modify the corresponding environment variables to point to the locations
inside the image.
It will also mount APIVFS filesystems (\f[B]/proc\f[R], \f[B]/dev\f[R],
\&...)
to make sure scripts and tools executed inside the chroot work properly.
It also propagates \f[B]/etc/resolv.conf\f[R] from the host into the
chroot if requested so that DNS resolution works inside the chroot.
After the mkosi\-chroot command exits, various mount points are cleaned
up.
.RS 2
.PP
For example, to invoke \f[B]ls\f[R] inside of the image, use the
following:
.IP
.EX
mkosi\-chroot ls ...
.EE
.PP
To execute the entire script inside the image, add a \f[B].chroot\f[R]
suffix to the name (\f[B]mkosi.build.chroot\f[R] instead of
\f[B]mkosi.build\f[R], etc.).
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
For all of the supported package managers (\f[B]dnf\f[R], \f[B]rpm\f[R],
\f[B]apt\f[R], \f[B]dpkg\f[R], \f[B]pacman\f[R], \f[B]zypper\f[R]),
scripts of the same name are put into \f[B]$PATH\f[R] that make sure
these commands operate on the image\(cqs root directory with the
configuration supplied by the user instead of on the host system.
This means that from a script, you can do e.g.
\f[B]dnf install vim\f[R] to install vim into the image.
.RS 2
.PP
Additionally, \f[B]mkosi\-install\f[R], \f[B]mkosi\-reinstall\f[R],
\f[B]mkosi\-upgrade\f[R] and \f[B]mkosi\-remove\f[R] will invoke the
corresponding operation of the package manager being used to built the
image.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]git\f[R] is automatically invoked with \f[B]safe.directory=*\f[R]
to avoid permissions errors when running as the root user in a user
namespace.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]useradd\f[R] and \f[B]groupadd\f[R] are automatically invoked with
\f[B]\-\-root=$BUILDROOT\f[R] when executed outside of the image.
.PP
When scripts are executed, any directories that are still writable are
also made read\-only (\f[B]/home\f[R], \f[B]/var\f[R], \f[B]/root\f[R],
\&...)
and only the minimal set of directories that need to be writable remain
writable.
This is to ensure that scripts can\(cqt mess with the host system when
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] is running as root.
.PP
Note that when executing scripts, all source directories are made
ephemeral which means all changes made to source directories while
running scripts are thrown away after the scripts finish executing.
Use the output, build or cache directories if you need to persist data
between builds.
.SH "فایل‌ها (FILES)"
To make it easy to build images for development versions of your
projects, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] can read configuration data from the local
directory, under the assumption that it is invoked from a
\f[I]source\f[R] tree.
Specifically, the following files are used if they exist in the local
directory:
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.skeleton/\f[B]\f[R] directory or
\f[B]\f[R]mkosi.skeleton.tar\f[B]\f[R] archive may be used to insert
files into the image.
The files are copied \f[I]before\f[R] the distribution packages are
installed into the image.
This allows creation of files that need to be provided early, for
example to configure the package manager or set systemd presets.
.RS 2
.PP
When using the directory, file ownership is not preserved: all files
copied will be owned by root.
To preserve ownership, use a tar archive.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.extra/\f[B]\f[R] directory or
\f[B]\f[R]mkosi.extra.tar\f[B]\f[R] archive may be used to insert
additional files into the image, on top of what the distribution
includes in its packages.
They are similar to \f[B]mkosi.skeleton/\f[R] and
\f[B]mkosi.skeleton.tar\f[R], but the files are copied into the
directory tree of the image \f[I]after\f[R] the OS was installed.
.RS 2
.PP
When using the directory, file ownership is not preserved: all files
copied will be owned by root.
To preserve ownership, use a tar archive.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.sandbox/\f[B]\f[R] directory or
\f[B]\f[R]mkosi.sandbox.tar\f[B]\f[R] archive may be used to configure
the package manager without the files being inserted into the image.
If the files should be included in the image \f[B]mkosi.skeleton/\f[R]
and \f[B]mkosi.skeleton.tar\f[R] should be used instead.
.RS 2
.PP
When using the directory, file ownership is not preserved: all files
copied will be owned by root.
To preserve ownership, use a tar archive.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.nspawn\f[B]\f[R] nspawn settings file will be copied
into the same place as the output image file, if it exists.
This is useful since nspawn looks for settings files next to image files
it boots, for additional container runtime settings.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.cache/\f[B]\f[R] directory, if it exists, is
automatically used as package download cache, in order to speed repeated
runs of the tool.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.builddir/\f[B]\f[R] directory, if it exists, is
automatically used as out\-of\-tree build directory, if the build
commands in the \f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] scripts support it.
Specifically, this directory will be mounted into the build container,
and the \f[B]$BUILDDIR\f[R] environment variable will be set to it when
the build scripts are invoked.
A build script may then use this directory as build directory, for
\f[B]automake\f[R]\-style or \f[B]ninja\f[R]\-style out\-of\-tree
builds.
This speeds up builds considerably, in particular when \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
is used in incremental mode (\f[B]\-i\f[R]): not only the image and
build overlay, but also the build tree is reused between subsequent
invocations.
Note that if this directory does not exist the \f[B]$BUILDDIR\f[R]
environment variable is not set, and it is up to the build scripts to
decide whether to do an in\-tree or an out\-of\-tree build, and which
build directory to use.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.rootpw\f[B]\f[R] file can be used to provide the
password for the root user of the image.
If the password is prefixed with \f[B]hashed:\f[R] it is treated as an
already hashed root password.
The password may optionally be followed by a newline character which is
implicitly removed.
The file must have an access mode of 0600 or less.
If this file does not exist, the distribution\(cqs default root password
is set (which usually means access to the root user is blocked).
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.passphrase\f[B]\f[R] file provides the passphrase to
use when LUKS encryption is selected.
It should contain the passphrase literally, and not end in a newline
character (i.e.\ in the same format as \f[B]cryptsetup\f[R] and
\f[B]/etc/crypttab\f[R] expect the passphrase files).
The file must have an access mode of 0600 or less.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.crt\f[B]\f[R] and \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.key\f[B]\f[R]
files contain an X.509 certificate and PEM private key to use when
signing is required (UEFI SecureBoot, verity, \&...).
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.output/\f[B]\f[R] directory is used to store all
build artifacts.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.credentials/\f[B]\f[R] directory is used as a source
of extra credentials similar to the \f[B]Credentials=\f[R] option.
For each file in the directory, the filename will be used as the
credential name and the file contents become the credential value, or,
if the file is executable, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will execute the file and the
command\(cqs output to stdout will be used as the credential value.
Output to stderr will be ignored.
Credentials configured with \f[B]Credentials=\f[R] take precedence over
files in \f[B]mkosi.credentials\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[B]\f[R]mkosi.repart/\f[B]\f[R] directory is used as the source
for \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] partition definition files which are
passed to \f[B]systemd\-repart\f[R] when building a disk image.
If it does not exist and the \f[B]RepartDirectories=\f[R] setting is not
configured, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will default to the following partition
definition files:
.RS 2
.PP
\f[B]00\-esp.conf\f[R] (if we\(cqre building a bootable image):
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Partition]\f[R]
Type=esp
Format=vfat
CopyFiles=/boot:/
CopyFiles=/efi:/
SizeMinBytes=512M
SizeMaxBytes=512M
.EE
.PP
\f[B]05\-bios.conf\f[R] (if we\(cqre building a BIOS bootable image):
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Partition]\f[R]
\f[I]# UUID of the grub BIOS boot partition which grubs needs on GPT to\f[R]
\f[I]# embed itself into.\f[R]
Type=21686148\-6449\-6e6f\-744e\-656564454649
SizeMinBytes=1M
SizeMaxBytes=1M
.EE
.PP
\f[B]10\-root.conf\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Partition]\f[R]
Type=root
Format=<distribution\-default\-filesystem>
CopyFiles=/
Minimize=guess
.EE
.PP
Note that if either \f[B]mkosi.repart/\f[R] is found or
\f[B]RepartDirectories=\f[R] is used, we will not use any of the default
partition definitions.
.RE
.PP
All these files are optional.
.PP
Note that the location of all these files may also be configured during
invocation via command line switches, and as settings in
\f[B]mkosi.conf\f[R], in case the default settings are not acceptable
for a project.
.SH CACHING
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] supports three different caches for speeding up
repetitive re\-building of images.
Specifically:
.IP "1." 3
The package cache of the distribution package manager is cached between
builds.
The location can beconfigured with the \f[B]PackageCacheDirectory=\f[R]
option or the \f[B]mkosi.pkgcache/\f[R] directory.
This form of caching relies on the distribution\(cqs package manager,
and caches distribution packages (RPM, deb, \&...)
after they are downloaded, but before they are unpacked.
.IP "2." 3
The repository metadata of the distribution is cached if a cache
directory is configured with \f[B]CacheDirectory=\f[R] or the
\f[B]mkosi.cache/\f[R] directory.
When the repository metadata is resynced is controlled with the
\f[B]CacheOnly=\f[R] option, which by default will always resync the
repository metadata unless every image is cached (see the
\f[B]Incremental=\f[R] option).
Whenever the repository metadata is resynced, all cached images are
invalidated.
.IP "3." 3
If the incremental build mode is enabled with \f[B]Incremental=yes\f[R],
cached copies of the final image and build overlay are made immediately
before the build sources are copied in (for the build overlay) or the
artifacts generated by \f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] are copied in (in case of
the final image).
This form of caching allows bypassing the time\-consuming package
unpacking step of the distribution package managers, but is only
effective if the list of packages to use remains stable, but the build
sources and its scripts change regularly.
.IP "4." 3
Finally, between multiple builds the build artifact directory may be
shared, using the \f[B]BuildDirectory=\f[R] option or the
\f[B]mkosi.builddir/\f[R] directory.
This directory allows build systems such as Meson to reuse already
compiled sources from a previous build, thus speeding up the build
process of a \f[B]mkosi.build\f[R] build script.
.SH TOOLS TREES
Tools trees are a secondary image that mkosi can use to build the actual
images.
This is useful to make image builds more reproducible, but also allows
to use newer tooling, that is not yet available in the host distribution
running mkosi.
.PP
Tools trees can be provided via the \f[B]ToolsTree=\f[R] option, the
\f[B]mkosi.tools\f[R] directory or built automatically by mkosi if set
to \f[B]ToolsTree=yes\f[R].
For most use cases setting it is sufficient to use the default tools
trees and the use of a tools tree is recommended.
.PP
Fully custom tools trees can be built like any other mkosi image, but
mkosi provides a builtin include providing the default tools tree
packages:
.IP
.EX
mkosi \-\-include=mkosi\-tools \-\-format=directory
.EE
.PP
Tools trees, including default tools trees, can be further customized
via the different \f[B]ToolsTree*=\f[R] variables as well as the
\f[B]mkosi.tools.conf\f[R] configuration file or directory.
The output format for tools trees cannot currently be changed via
configuration files.
.PP
The following table shows for which distributions default tools tree
packages are defined and which packages are included in those default
tools trees:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(18.8n) cw(6.0n) cw(6.0n) cw(6.0n) cw(4.5n) cw(6.0n) cw(4.5n) cw(7.5n) cw(10.5n).
