.TE
.SH BUILDING MULTIPLE IMAGES
If the \f[B]mkosi.images/\f[R] directory exists, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will
load individual subimage configurations from it and build each of them.
Image configurations can be either directories containing
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] configuration files or regular files with the
\f[B].conf\f[R] extension.
.PP
When image configurations are found in \f[B]mkosi.images/\f[R],
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] will build the images specified in the
\f[B]Dependencies=\f[R] setting of the main image and all of their
dependencies (or all of them if no images were explicitly configured
using \f[B]Dependencies=\f[R] in the main image configuration).
To add dependencies between subimages, the \f[B]Dependencies=\f[R]
setting can be used as well.
Subimages are always built before the main image.
.PP
When images are defined, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will first read the main image
configuration (configuration outside of the \f[B]mkosi.images/\f[R]
directory), followed by the image specific configuration.
.PP
Several \(lqmultiversal\(rq settings apply to the default tools tree and
to the main image and cannot be configured separately outside of the
main image:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]RepositoryKeyCheck=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]RepositoryKeyFetch=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SourceDateEpoch=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]WorkspaceDirectory=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]PackageCacheDirectory=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]BuildSources=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]BuildSourcesEphemeral=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ProxyClientCertificate=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ProxyClientKey=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ProxyExclude=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ProxyPeerCertificate=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ProxyUrl=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]MakeScriptsExecutable=\f[R]
.PP
Several \(lquniversal\(rq settings apply to the main image and all its
subimages and cannot be configured separately in subimages.
The following settings are universal and cannot be configured in
subimages:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Architecture=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]BuildDirectory=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CacheDirectory=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Distribution=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ExtraSearchPaths=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]LocalMirror=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Mirror=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]OutputDirectory=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]OutputMode=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]PackageDirectories=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Release=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]RepartOffline=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Repositories=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SandboxTrees=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTree=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeCertificates=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]UseSubvolumes=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SecureBootCertificate=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SecureBootCertificateSource=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SecureBootKey=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SecureBootKeySource=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]VerityCertificate=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]VerityCertificateSource=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]VerityKey=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]VerityKeySource=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]VolatilePackageDirectories=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]WithNetwork=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]WithTests\f[R]
.PP
There are also settings which are passed down to subimages but can be
overridden.
For these settings, values configured explicitly in the subimage will
take priority over values configured on the CLI or in the main image
config.
Currently the following settings are passed down to subimages but can be
overridden:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Incremental=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CacheOnly=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Profiles=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ImageId=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ImageVersion=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SectorSize=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CacheKey=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]BuildKey=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CompressLevel=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrKey=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrKeySource=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrCertificate=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SignExpectedPcrCertificateSource=\f[R]
.PP
Additionally, there are various settings that can only be configured in
the main image but which are not passed down to subimages:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]MinimumVersion=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]PassEnvironment=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeDistribution=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeRelease=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeProfiles=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeMirror=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeSnapshot=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeRepositories=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreeSandboxTrees=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreePackages=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ToolsTreePackageDirectories=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]History=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Every setting in the \f[B][Runtime]\f[R] section
.PP
Images can refer to outputs of images they depend on.
Specifically, for the following options, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will only check
whether the inputs exist just before building the image:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]BaseTrees=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ExtraTrees=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Initrds=\f[R]
.PP
To refer to outputs of a image\(cqs dependencies, simply configure any
of these options with a relative path to the output to use in the output
directory of the dependency.
Or use the \f[B]%O\f[R] specifier to refer to the output directory.
.PP
A good example on how to build multiple images can be found in the \c
.UR https://github.com/systemd/systemd/tree/main/mkosi/mkosi.images
systemd
.UE \c
\ repository.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_LESS\f[R] overrides options for \f[B]less\f[R] when it is
invoked by \f[B]mkosi\f[R] to page output.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_DNF\f[R] can be used to override the executable used as
\f[B]dnf\f[R].
This is particularly useful to select between \f[B]dnf\f[R] and
\f[B]dnf5\f[R].
Note that this variable is only honoured when no tools tree is in use;
when a tools tree is active, the dnf executable is autodetected from
within the tools tree.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_DNF_DISABLE_PLUGINS\f[R] can be used to disable dnf plugins.
The value is forwarded to dnf\(cqs \f[B]\-\-disableplugin=\f[R] option.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$MKOSI_DNF_ENABLE_PLUGINS\f[R] can be used to enable dnf plugins.
The value is forwarded to dnf\(cqs \f[B]\-\-enableplugin=\f[R] option.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]$EPEL_MIRROR\f[R] can be used to override the default mirror
location used for the epel repositories when \f[B]Mirror=\f[R] is used.
By default \f[B]mkosi\f[R] looks for the epel repositories in the
\f[B]fedora\f[R] subdirectory of the parent directory of the mirror
specified in \f[B]Mirror=\f[R].
For example if the mirror is set to
\f[B]https://mirror.net/centos\-stream\f[R] \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will look
for the epel repositories in \f[B]https://mirror.net/fedora/epel\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]SYSEXT_SCOPE\f[R] and \f[B]CONFEXT_SCOPE\f[R] can be used to
override the default value of the respective
\f[B]extension\-release\f[R] file when building a sysext or confext.
By default the value is set to \f[B]initrd system portable\f[R].
.SH "مثال‌ها (EXAMPLES)"
Create and run a raw \f[I]GPT\f[R] image with \f[I]ext4\f[R], as
\f[B]image.raw\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
# mkosi \-p systemd \-i boot
.EE
.PP
Create and run a bootable \f[I]GPT\f[R] image, as \f[B]foobar.raw\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ mkosi \-d fedora \-p kernel\-core \-p systemd \-p systemd\-boot \-p udev \-o foobar.raw
# mkosi \-\-output foobar.raw boot
$ mkosi \-\-output foobar.raw vm
.EE
.PP
Create and run a \f[I]Fedora Linux\f[R] image in a plain directory:
.IP
.EX
# mkosi \-\-distribution fedora \-\-format directory boot
.EE
.PP
Create a compressed image \f[B]image.raw.xz\f[R] with SSH installed and
add a checksum file:
.IP
.EX
$ mkosi \-\-distribution fedora \-\-format disk \-\-checksum=yes \-\-compress\-output=yes \-\-package=openssh\-clients
.EE
.PP
Inside the source directory of an \f[B]automake\f[R]\-based project,
configure \f[B]mkosi\f[R] so that simply invoking \f[B]mkosi\f[R]
without any parameters builds an OS image containing a built version of
the project in its current state:
.IP
.EX
$ cat >mkosi.conf <<EOF
[Distribution]
Distribution=fedora

[Output]
Format=disk

[Content]
Packages=kernel,systemd,systemd\-udev,openssh\-clients,httpd
BuildPackages=make,gcc,libcurl\-devel
EOF
$ cat >mkosi.build <<EOF
#!/bin/sh

if [ \(dq$container\(dq != \(dqmkosi\(dq ]; then
    exec mkosi\-chroot \(dq$CHROOT_SCRIPT\(dq \(dq$\(at\(dq
fi

cd $SRCDIR
\&./autogen.sh
\&./configure \-\-prefix=/usr
make \-j \(ganproc\(ga
make install
EOF
$ chmod +x mkosi.build
# mkosi \-i boot
# systemd\-nspawn \-bi image.raw
.EE
.SS Different ways to boot with \f[B]vm\f[R]
The easiest way to boot a virtual machine is to build an image with the
required components and let \f[B]mkosi\f[R] call \f[B]qemu\f[R] with all
the right options:
.IP
.EX
$ mkosi \-d fedora \-p systemd\-udev,systemd\-boot,kernel\-core build
$ mkosi \-d fedora vm
\&...
fedora login: root (automatic login)
[root\(atfedora \(ti]#
.EE
.PP
The default is to boot with a text console only.
In this mode, messages from the boot loader, the kernel, and systemd,
and later the \f[B]getty\f[R] login prompt and shell all use the same
terminal.
It is possible to switch between the \f[B]qemu\f[R] console and monitor
by pressing \f[B]Ctrl\-a c\f[R].
The \f[B]qemu\f[R] monitor may for example be used to inject special
keys or shut down the machine quickly.
Alternatively the machine can be shut down using \f[B]Ctrl\-a x\f[R].
.PP
To boot with a graphical window, add \f[B]\-\-console=gui\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ mkosi \-d fedora \-\-console=gui qemu
.EE
.PP
A kernel may be booted directly with \f[B]mkosi vm \-kernel ...
\-initrd ...
\-append \(aq...\(aq\f[R].
This is a bit faster because no boot loader is used, and it is also
easier to experiment with different kernels and kernel command lines.
Note that despite the name, \f[B]qemu\f[R]\(cqs \f[B]\-append\f[R]
option replaces the default kernel command line embedded in the kernel
and any previous \f[B]\-append\f[R] specifications.
.PP
The UKI is also copied into the output directory and may be booted
directly:
.IP
.EX
$ mkosi vm \-\- \-kernel mkosi.output/fedora\(ti38/image.efi
.EE
.PP
When booting using an external kernel, we don\(cqt need the kernel
\f[I]in\f[R] the image, but we would still want the kernel modules to be
installed.
.PP
It is also possible to do a \f[I]direct kernel boot\f[R] into a boot
loader, taking advantage of the fact that \f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R](7) is
a valid UEFI binary:
.IP
.EX
$ mkosi vm \-\- \-kernel /usr/lib/systemd/boot/efi/systemd\-bootx64.efi
.EE
.PP
In this scenario, the kernel is loaded from the ESP in the image by
\f[B]systemd\-boot\f[R].
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
mkosi requires a Linux kernel that provides \f[B]mount_setattr()\f[R]
which was introduces in 5.12.
.IP \(bu 2
mkosi currently requires systemd 254 or newer to build bootable disk
images.
.IP \(bu 2
mkosi requires various dependencies to be installed.
If a tools tree is used, (see the \f[B]ToolsTree=\f[R] documentation for
more information), then mkosi will download all of its dependencies
(except python and a package manager) itself.
Otherwise, you\(cqll need to install the required dependencies on your
system.
You can run \f[B]mkosi dependencies\f[R] to get a list of the basic
required dependencies.
For regular mkosi use, we strongly recommend using a tools tree.
.IP \(bu 2
On Debian/Kali/Ubuntu it might be necessary to install the
\f[B]ubuntu\-keyring\f[R], \f[B]ubuntu\-archive\-keyring\f[R],
\f[B]kali\-archive\-keyring\f[R] and/or
\f[B]debian\-archive\-keyring\f[R] packages explicitly, in addition to
\f[B]apt\f[R], depending on what kind of distribution images you want to
build.
.IP \(bu 2
The minimum required Python version is 3.9.
.SS Unprivileged User Namespaces
mkosi needs unrestricted abilities to create and act within Linux
namespaces.
Some distributions restrict the creation of, or capabilities within,
user namespaces, which breaks mkosi.
.PP
For information about Ubuntu, which implements such restrictions using
AppArmor, see
https://ubuntu.com/blog/ubuntu\-23\-10\-restricted\-unprivileged\-user\-namespaces.
To enable unprivileged user namespaces on Ubuntu, run the following
commands:
.IP
.EX
sudo sysctl \-w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined=0
sudo sysctl \-w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0
.EE
.PP
To persist these sysctl settings across reboots, create
\f[B]/etc/sysctl.d/unprivileged\-userns.conf\f[R] with the following
contents:
.IP
.EX
kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined=0
kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0
.EE
.PP
For other systems, try researching the
\f[B]kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone\f[R] or
\f[B]user.max.user_namespace\f[R] sysctls.
.SH FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)
.IP \(bu 2
Why does \f[B]mkosi vm\f[R] with KVM not work on Debian/Kali/Ubuntu?
.RS 2
.PP
While other distributions are OK with allowing access to
\f[B]/dev/kvm\f[R], on Debian/Kali/Ubuntu this is only allowed for users
in the \f[B]kvm\f[R] group.
Because \f[B]mkosi\f[R] unshares a user namespace when running
unprivileged, even if the calling user was in the kvm group, when
\f[B]mkosi\f[R] unshares the user namespace to run unprivileged, it
loses access to the \f[B]kvm\f[R] group and by the time we start
\f[B]qemu\f[R] we don\(cqt have access to \f[B]/dev/kvm\f[R] anymore.
As a workaround, you can change the permissions of the device nodes to
\f[B]0666\f[R] which is sufficient to make KVM work unprivileged.
To persist these settings across reboots, copy
\f[B]/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/static\-nodes\-permissions.conf\f[R] to
\f[B]/etc/tmpfiles.d/static\-nodes\-permissions.conf\f[R] and change the
mode of \f[B]/dev/kvm\f[R] from \f[B]0660\f[R] to \f[B]0666\f[R].
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
How do I add a regular user to an image?
.RS 2
.PP
You can use the following snippet in a post\-installation script:
.IP
.EX
useradd \-\-create\-home \-\-user\-group $USER \-\-password \(dq$(openssl passwd \-stdin \-6 <$USER_PASSWORD_FILE)\(dq
.EE
.PP
Note that from systemd v256 onwards, if enabled,
\f[B]systemd\-homed\-firstboot.service\f[R] will prompt to create a
regular user on first boot if there are no regular users.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Why do I see failures to chown files when building images?
.RS 2
.PP
When not running as root, your user is not able to change ownership of
files to arbitrary owners.
Various distributions still ship files in their packages that are not
owned by the root user.
When not running as root, mkosi maps the current user to root when
invoking package managers, which means that changing ownership to root
will work but changing ownership to any other user or group will fail.
.PP
Note that chown calls are only suppressed when running package managers,
but not when running scripts.
If this is required, e.g.\ for a build script, you can set the
\f[B]MKOSI_CHROOT_SUPPRESS_CHOWN\f[R] variable to a true value
(\f[B]1\f[R], \f[B]yes\f[R], \f[B]true\f[R]) to suppress chown calls in
\f[B]mkosi\-chroot\f[R] and \f[B].chroot\f[R] scripts.
.PP
If this behavior causes applications running in your image to misbehave,
you can consider running \f[B]mkosi\f[R] as root which avoids this
problem.
Alternatively, if running \f[B]mkosi\f[R] as root is not desired, you
can use \f[B]unshare \-\-map\-auto \-\-map\-current\-user \-\-setuid 0
\-\-setgid 0\f[R] to become root in a user namespace with more than one
user assuming the UID/GID mappings in \f[B]/etc/subuid\f[R] and
\f[B]/etc/subgid\f[R] are configured correctly.
Note that running mkosi as root or with \f[B]unshare\f[R] means that all
output files produced by \f[B]mkosi\f[R] will not be owned by your
current user anymore.
.PP
Note that for systemd services that need directories in \f[B]/var\f[R]
owned by the service user and group, an alternative to shipping these
directories in packages or creating them via systemd\-tmpfiles is to use
\f[B]StateDirectory=\f[R], \f[B]CacheDirectory=\f[R] or
\f[B]LogsDirectory=\f[R] in the service file which instructs systemd to
create the directory when it first starts the service.
.PP
Alternatively, the \f[B]z\f[R] or \f[B]Z\f[R] directives for
\f[B]systemd\-tmpfiles\f[R] can be used to chown various directories and
files to their owning user when the system first boots up.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Why does \f[B]portablectl inspect <image>\f[R]/\f[B]systemd\-dissect
<image>\f[R] say my portable service isn\(cqt one?
.RS 2
.PP
\f[B]systemd\-dissect\f[R] and\f[B]portablectl inspect\f[R] check for
\f[B]PORTABLE_PREFIXES=\f[R] in \f[B]os\-release\f[R] and if the key is
missing, will fail to recognise a portable service as one, showing ✗
under \f[I]Use as\f[R] for in the case of \f[B]systemd\-dissect\f[R] or
\f[B]n/a\f[R] under \f[I]Portable Service\f[R] for
\f[B]portablectl\f[R].
.PP
Since there is no good default to set for this key and the generated
portable service images will still attach properly, even when the key is
not set, \f[B]mkosi\f[R] doesn\(cqt set one.
.PP
You can set \f[B]PORTABLE_PREFIXES=\f[R] by setting
\f[B]Environment=PORTABLE_PREFIXES=XXX\f[R] in your mkosi config.
.RE
